LESSONS = []

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 1. SHM: the defining equation
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Simple harmonic motion",
    title="Simple harmonic motion: the defining equation",
    slug="phl2-shm-defining-equation",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Define simple harmonic motion by a = −ω²x, derive x = A sin ωt from the reference circle, and find speed, acceleration, period and frequency.",
    objectives=[
        "Define simple harmonic motion and explain every part of a = −ω²x",
        "Derive x = A sin ωt, v = ωA cos ωt and a = −ω²A sin ωt from the reference circle",
        "Use v = ±ω√(A² − x²) to find the speed at any displacement",
        "State where speed and acceleration are largest and where they are zero",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Oscillation", "Amplitude", "Period and frequency", "Angular frequency", "Simple harmonic motion", "Reference circle"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A bus bounces on its springs after a pothole on the road to Bamenda. A mango hangs from a branch and swings in the wind. A guitar string vibrates after it is plucked. All of these **oscillate**: they move to and fro about a fixed position. The simplest and most important oscillation is called **simple harmonic motion**, or SHM. Examiners say it was covered too lightly in many schools, so we will build it slowly, from the definition to the equations."),
        H("Words you must know"),
        KEY("Describing any oscillation", r"Let the **equilibrium position** be the place where the object would rest if it were not oscillating.", cards=[
            ["Displacement x", "The distance of the object from the equilibrium position, in a stated direction. It can be positive or negative."],
            ["Amplitude A", "The maximum displacement from equilibrium. It is always positive."],
            ["Period T", "The time for one complete oscillation, in seconds."],
            ["Frequency f", r"The number of oscillations each second, in hertz (Hz). $f = \dfrac{1}{T}$."],
            [r"Angular frequency $\omega$", r"$\omega = 2\pi f = \dfrac{2\pi}{T}$, in radians per second. It is not the same as $f$."],
        ]),
        H("The definition of simple harmonic motion"),
        D(r"An object performs **simple harmonic motion** when its acceleration is directly proportional to its displacement from a fixed point (the equilibrium position) and is always directed towards that point.", title="Definition of SHM",
          formula=r"a = -\omega^2 x",
          note=r"Here $\omega^2$ is the constant of proportionality. The minus sign means that $a$ and $x$ point in opposite directions."),
        T(r"Both parts of the definition matter. If an acceleration is proportional to displacement but points **away** from equilibrium, the object runs away and does not oscillate. If it points towards equilibrium but is not proportional to $x$, the motion is periodic but it is not SHM."),
        T(r"Think about a mass on a spring. Pull it to the right ($x > 0$) and the spring pulls it left, so $a < 0$. Push it to the left ($x < 0$) and the spring pushes it right, so $a > 0$. The sign of $a$ is always opposite to the sign of $x$. The stronger the stretch, the larger the pull, so $|a|$ grows with $|x|$."),
        H("Where the sine curve comes from: the reference circle"),
        T(r"We now show that $a = -\omega^2 x$ leads to a sine wave. The neatest method uses circular motion. A point P moves round a circle of radius $A$ at constant angular speed $\omega$. Let $N$ be the foot of the perpendicular from P to a horizontal diameter. As P goes round, N moves to and fro along the diameter, like an object in SHM."),
        IMG("al-phl2-reference-circle.svg", alt="A circle of radius A with centre O. Point P is at angle omega t from the vertical. N is the foot of the perpendicular from P on the horizontal diameter, at distance x from O. The velocity of P is a tangent arrow of size omega A.", caption="Figure 1. The reference circle. The shadow N of P moves along the diameter in SHM."),
        T(r"Measure the angle $\omega t$ from the vertical, so that at $t = 0$ the point P is at the top and N is at the centre O. After time $t$ the angle is $\omega t$, and the displacement of N from O is"),
        F(r"x = A\sin\omega t", title="Displacement"),
        T(r"The speed of P round the circle is $\omega A$, along the tangent. The tangent makes angle $\omega t$ with the horizontal, so its horizontal component is $\omega A\cos\omega t$. This is the velocity of N:"),
        F(r"v = \frac{dx}{dt} = \omega A\cos\omega t", title="Velocity"),
        T(r"P has an acceleration $\omega^2 A$ directed towards the centre O (this is centripetal acceleration, from circular motion). Its horizontal component, pointing towards O, is $\omega^2 A\sin\omega t$ in the direction opposite to $x$. So the acceleration of N is"),
        F(r"a = \frac{dv}{dt} = -\omega^2 A\sin\omega t = -\omega^2 x", title="Acceleration"),
        T(r"This is exactly the defining equation. So N, and any object whose acceleration obeys $a = -\omega^2 x$, performs SHM with displacement $x = A\sin\omega t$. If the motion starts at maximum displacement instead, use $x = A\cos\omega t$. Both are the same curve, shifted along the time axis."),
        H("Speed at any displacement"),
        T(r"Often the question gives a displacement, not a time. Eliminate $t$ using $\sin^2\omega t + \cos^2\omega t = 1$:"),
        F(r"v^2 = \omega^2A^2\cos^2\omega t = \omega^2A^2\left(1 - \sin^2\omega t\right) = \omega^2\left(A^2 - x^2\right)"),
        D(r"The speed at displacement $x$ is given by", title="Speed formula",
          formula=r"v = \pm\omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}",
          note="The plus sign is for motion in one direction and the minus sign for the other direction. The speed is the positive value."),
        KEY("Largest and smallest values", "Learn where each quantity is largest. The graphs in the next lesson show the same facts.", cards=[
            [r"At equilibrium ($x = 0$)", r"Speed is **maximum**, $v_{max} = \omega A$. Acceleration is **zero**."],
            [r"At the ends ($x = \pm A$)", r"Speed is **zero** (the object turns round). Acceleration is **maximum**, $a_{max} = \omega^2 A$, directed towards the centre."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: period, maximum speed and acceleration", intro=r"A mass on a spring in a school laboratory oscillates in SHM with frequency $2.5\ \text{Hz}$ in a slow-motion demonstration model, and amplitude $4.0\ \text{cm}$. Find the period, $\omega$, the maximum speed and the maximum acceleration.", steps=[
            S("Period from frequency", math=r"T = \frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{2.5} = 0.40\ \text{s}"),
            S("Angular frequency", math=r"\omega = 2\pi f = 2\pi \times 2.5 = 15.7\ \text{rad s}^{-1}"),
            S("Maximum speed (change cm to m first: $A = 0.040\\ \\text{m}$)", math=r"v_{max} = \omega A = 15.7 \times 0.040 = 0.63\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Maximum acceleration", math=r"a_{max} = \omega^2A = 15.7^2 \times 0.040 = 9.9\ \text{m s}^{-2}"),
        ], check=r"\omega A = 0.6283,\quad \omega^2A = 9.870"),
        EX("Worked example 2: speed and acceleration at a given displacement", intro=r"An object oscillates in SHM with $\omega = 12\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$ and amplitude $5.0\ \text{cm}$. Find its speed and acceleration when its displacement is $3.0\ \text{cm}$.", steps=[
            S("Convert to metres", text=r"$A = 0.050\ \text{m}$ and $x = 0.030\ \text{m}$."),
            S("Speed", math=r"v = \omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2} = 12\sqrt{0.050^2 - 0.030^2} = 12 \times 0.040 = 0.48\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Acceleration", math=r"a = -\omega^2x = -12^2 \times 0.030 = -4.3\ \text{m s}^{-2}"),
            S("Say what the sign means", text="The acceleration has magnitude 4.3 m/s squared and points towards the equilibrium position."),
        ], check=r"v^2 = 144(0.0025 - 0.0009) = 0.2304 = 0.48^2"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a time from the equation", intro=r"The period of an SHM is $1.2\ \text{s}$. The motion starts at equilibrium. How long does the object take to reach a displacement $\frac{A}{2}$? How long from $\frac{A}{2}$ to the end point $A$?", steps=[
            S("Angular frequency", math=r"\omega = \frac{2\pi}{1.2} = 5.24\ \text{rad s}^{-1}"),
            S(r"Put $x = \frac{A}{2}$ into $x = A\sin\omega t$", math=r"\sin\omega t = \tfrac{1}{2} \;\Rightarrow\; \omega t = \frac{\pi}{6}"),
            S("Solve for t (use radians)", math=r"t = \frac{\pi/6}{5.24} = 0.10\ \text{s}"),
            S(r"Reaching the end point $A$ takes a quarter period, $\frac{T}{4} = 0.30\ \text{s}$", math=r"0.30 - 0.10 = 0.20\ \text{s}"),
            S("Comment", text="The second half of the way takes twice as long as the first half, because the object is slowing down near the end."),
        ], check=r"t_1 = T/12 = 0.10,\quad T/4 - T/12 = T/6 = 0.20"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using degrees in the calculator", r"The formulas $x = A\sin\omega t$ and $v = \omega A\cos\omega t$ need $\omega t$ in **radians**. Set the calculator to radian mode before you evaluate a sine or cosine."),
        MISTAKE(r"Mixing up $\omega$ and $f$", r"$\omega = 2\pi f$. If $a = -25x$, then $\omega^2 = 25$, so $\omega = 5\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$ and $f = \dfrac{5}{2\pi} = 0.80\ \text{Hz}$, not $5\ \text{Hz}$."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking the speed is largest at the ends", "At the ends the object stops for an instant, so the speed is zero there. The speed is largest at the equilibrium position, where the acceleration is zero."),
        MISTAKE("Dropping the minus sign in the definition", r"The acceleration points towards equilibrium. Writing $a = +\omega^2 x$ describes an object that is pushed away from equilibrium, which is not SHM."),
        QUIZ(r"A body moves with acceleration $a = -25x$ in SI units. What is its frequency?",
             [r"$0.80\ \text{Hz}$", r"$5.0\ \text{Hz}$", r"$25\ \text{Hz}$", r"$0.20\ \text{Hz}$"], 0,
             r"Compare with $a = -\omega^2x$: $\omega^2 = 25$, so $\omega = 5.0\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$. Then $f = \dfrac{\omega}{2\pi} = 0.80\ \text{Hz}$.",
             hint=r"First find $\omega$, then convert it to $f$.",
             feedback=["", r"$5.0$ is $\omega$ in rad/s. You must divide by $2\pi$ to get hertz.", r"$25$ is $\omega^2$. Take the square root first, then divide by $2\pi$.", r"$0.20 = 1/\omega$. That is neither the period nor the frequency."]),
        QUIZ("Which row is correct for an object in SHM at its equilibrium position?",
             ["Speed zero, acceleration maximum", "Speed maximum, acceleration maximum", "Speed zero, acceleration zero", "Speed maximum, acceleration zero"], 3,
             r"At $x = 0$: $v = \omega A$ is maximum and $a = -\omega^2x = 0$.",
             hint=r"Use $a = -\omega^2x$ and $v = \omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$ with $x = 0$.",
             feedback=["That describes the end points, not the centre.", r"The acceleration is proportional to $x$, so it is zero when $x = 0$.", "The object is moving fastest through the centre; it is the end points where it stops.", ""]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A body performs SHM with angular frequency $8.0\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$ and amplitude $3.0\ \text{cm}$. Find the period, the maximum speed and the maximum acceleration.",
           r"$T = \dfrac{2\pi}{\omega} = \dfrac{2\pi}{8.0} = 0.79\ \text{s}$. $v_{max} = \omega A = 8.0 \times 0.030 = 0.24\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. $a_{max} = \omega^2A = 64 \times 0.030 = 1.9\ \text{m s}^{-2}$."),
        PQ(r"A particle oscillates in SHM with period $0.50\ \text{s}$ and amplitude $6.0\ \text{cm}$. Calculate its speed when its displacement is $4.0\ \text{cm}$.",
           r"$\omega = \dfrac{2\pi}{0.50} = 12.57\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$. $v = \omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2} = 12.57\sqrt{0.060^2 - 0.040^2} = 12.57 \times 0.04472 = 0.56\ \text{m s}^{-1}$."),
        PQ(r"Differentiate $x = A\sin\omega t$ twice with respect to time to show that it satisfies $a = -\omega^2x$.",
           r"$v = \dfrac{dx}{dt} = \omega A\cos\omega t$. $a = \dfrac{dv}{dt} = -\omega^2A\sin\omega t$. Since $x = A\sin\omega t$, this is $a = -\omega^2x$. The acceleration is proportional to the displacement and opposite in direction, so the motion is SHM."),
        PQ(r"An oscillator has speed $0.40\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ when its displacement is $0.030\ \text{m}$ and speed $0.30\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ when its displacement is $0.040\ \text{m}$. Find its angular frequency and amplitude.",
           r"$v^2 = \omega^2(A^2 - x^2)$. So $0.16 = \omega^2(A^2 - 0.0009)$ and $0.09 = \omega^2(A^2 - 0.0016)$. Subtract: $0.07 = \omega^2 \times 0.0007$, so $\omega^2 = 100$ and $\omega = 10\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$. Then $A^2 = 0.0016 + 0.0009 = 0.0025$, so $A = 0.050\ \text{m}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: SHM appears in Paper 1 (multiple choice, often on a = −ω²x and where speed is maximum) and in Paper 2 (structured questions with graphs and calculations). The Board's 2023 report noted that SHM was shallowly covered, so expect to explain as well as calculate. In the practical Paper 3 it appears through the pendulum and spring experiments."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"SHM: acceleration proportional to displacement from equilibrium and directed towards it, $a = -\omega^2x$.",
            r"$\omega = 2\pi f = \dfrac{2\pi}{T}$; solutions $x = A\sin\omega t$ (start at equilibrium) or $x = A\cos\omega t$ (start at an end).",
            r"$v = \omega A\cos\omega t$ and $v = \pm\omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2}$; $v_{max} = \omega A$ at $x = 0$.",
            r"$a_{max} = \omega^2A$ at $x = \pm A$, where the speed is zero.",
            "Always work in radians and in metres."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2. SHM graphs and phase
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Simple harmonic motion",
    title="SHM graphs, phase and phase difference",
    slug="phl2-shm-graphs-phase",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Read and sketch x–t, v–t, a–t, v–x and a–x graphs for SHM, use gradients to link them, and find phase differences between oscillations.",
    objectives=[
        "Sketch x, v and a against time and state their phase relationships",
        "Use the gradient of a graph to move from displacement to velocity to acceleration",
        "Interpret the v–x and a–x graphs of SHM",
        "Calculate a phase difference from a time difference and the period",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Displacement–time graph", "Gradient and rate of change", "Phase", "Phase difference", "Antiphase", "a–x graph"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Graphs are the favourite way for examiners to test SHM. A seismograph draws the displacement of the ground against time. A student in the school laboratory draws a graph of the position of a trolley on springs. If you can read amplitude, period, speed and acceleration from such a graph, you have understood SHM, not just memorised formulas."),
        H("The three graphs against time"),
        IMG("al-phl2-shm-graphs.svg", alt="Three stacked graphs against time over two periods. Displacement x is a sine curve from zero. Velocity v is a cosine curve starting at its maximum. Acceleration a is a negative sine curve. A dashed line at T/4 shows x at its maximum, v zero and a at its most negative value.", caption="Figure 1. For an oscillation that starts at equilibrium: x = A sin ωt, v = ωA cos ωt, a = −ω²A sin ωt."),
        T(r"All three are sinusoidal curves with the **same period** $T$. They differ in their amplitudes ($A$, $\omega A$, $\omega^2A$) and in where they start."),
        D(r"**Gradient rule.** The velocity is the gradient of the displacement–time graph, $v = \dfrac{dx}{dt}$. The acceleration is the gradient of the velocity–time graph, $a = \dfrac{dv}{dt}$.", title="Linking the graphs"),
        T(r"Check the rule on Figure 1. At $t = 0$ the $x$ graph is steepest, so $v$ is at its maximum. At $T/4$ the $x$ graph is at a peak and its gradient is zero, so $v = 0$. At that moment the $v$ graph is falling steeply, so $a$ is at its most negative value."),
        H("Phase relationships"),
        KEY("Three facts to learn", r"Taking $x = A\sin\omega t$:", cards=[
            [r"$v$ leads $x$ by $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$", r"The velocity graph reaches each peak a quarter of a period **before** the displacement graph does. We write $v = \omega A\sin\left(\omega t + \dfrac{\pi}{2}\right)$."],
            [r"$a$ and $x$ are in antiphase", r"A phase difference of $\pi$ (half a cycle): when $x$ is at its maximum, $a$ is at its most negative value."],
            [r"$a$ leads $v$ by $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$", r"The acceleration reaches its peaks a quarter period before the velocity does."],
        ]),
        D(r"The **phase** of an oscillation is the quantity $\omega t + \varphi$ in $x = A\sin(\omega t + \varphi)$, in radians. The **phase difference** between two oscillations of the same frequency is the angle $\Delta\varphi$ that tells how far one is ahead of the other in its cycle.", title="Phase and phase difference",
          formula=r"\Delta\varphi = 2\pi\,\frac{\Delta t}{T}",
          note=r"$\Delta t$ is the time by which one oscillation reaches a given point of its cycle after the other. One full period is $2\pi$ rad $= 360^\circ$."),
        H("The v–x and a–x graphs"),
        T(r"The graph of $a$ against $x$ is a **straight line through the origin** with negative gradient $-\omega^2$, because $a = -\omega^2x$. This is the quickest test for SHM and a good way to find $\omega$ from data."),
        T(r"The graph of $v$ against $x$ is an **ellipse**. From $v^2 = \omega^2(A^2 - x^2)$ we get $\dfrac{v^2}{(\omega A)^2} + \dfrac{x^2}{A^2} = 1$. It crosses the $x$ axis at $\pm A$ (where $v = 0$) and the $v$ axis at $\pm\omega A$ (where $x = 0$)."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reading an x–t graph", intro=r"A displacement–time graph of an oscillator is a sine curve. It starts from $x = 0$, first reaches its maximum of $6.0\ \text{cm}$ at $t = 0.20\ \text{s}$. Find the period, the equation of the motion, and the displacement, velocity and acceleration at $t = 0.50\ \text{s}$.", steps=[
            S("Period: from zero to the first maximum is one quarter of a period", math=r"T = 4 \times 0.20 = 0.80\ \text{s},\qquad \omega = \frac{2\pi}{0.80} = 7.85\ \text{rad s}^{-1}"),
            S("Equation (SI units, starts at 0)", math=r"x = 0.060\sin(7.85\,t)"),
            S("Displacement at $t = 0.50\\ \\text{s}$ (radians!)", math=r"x = 0.060\sin(3.927) = -0.042\ \text{m}"),
            S("Velocity", math=r"v = \omega A\cos\omega t = 7.85 \times 0.060\cos(3.927) = -0.33\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Acceleration", math=r"a = -\omega^2x = -(7.85)^2(-0.042) = +2.6\ \text{m s}^{-2}"),
            S("Check the signs", text="At 0.50 s the object is on the negative side and moving towards negative x, so v is negative. The acceleration points back to equilibrium, which is the positive direction."),
        ], check=r"\omega A = 0.4712,\quad \omega^2 A\sin(3.927) = -2.617"),
        EX("Worked example 2: using an a–x graph", intro=r"A student plots acceleration against displacement for a trolley held between two springs. The points lie on a straight line through the origin. The point $(0.040\ \text{m}, -1.0\ \text{m s}^{-2})$ is on the line. Find the period.", steps=[
            S("Gradient of the line", math=r"\frac{-1.0}{0.040} = -25\ \text{s}^{-2}"),
            S("Compare with $a = -\\omega^2x$", math=r"\omega^2 = 25 \Rightarrow \omega = 5.0\ \text{rad s}^{-1}"),
            S("Period", math=r"T = \frac{2\pi}{\omega} = 1.26\ \text{s}"),
        ], check=r"f = \frac{5.0}{2\pi} = 0.80\ \text{Hz},\quad T = \frac{1}{0.80} = 1.26\ \text{s}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: phase difference", intro=r"Two identical oscillators of period $0.60\ \text{s}$ are started so that oscillator B reaches its maximum $0.15\ \text{s}$ after oscillator A does. Find the phase difference.", steps=[
            S("Use $\\Delta\\varphi = 2\\pi\\,\\Delta t/T$", math=r"\Delta\varphi = 2\pi\times\frac{0.15}{0.60} = \frac{\pi}{2}\ \text{rad}"),
            S("In degrees", math=r"\frac{0.15}{0.60}\times 360^\circ = 90^\circ"),
            S("Say which leads", text="A is ahead. B lags A by a quarter of a cycle."),
        ], check=r"\frac{\pi}{2}\times\frac{180}{\pi} = 90"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Reading the gradient with the wrong sign", r"On an $x$–$t$ graph, positive gradient means $v > 0$. A peak has zero gradient, not maximum gradient. The steepest parts of the curve are where it crosses the time axis."),
        MISTAKE("Giving a phase difference in the wrong unit", r"State the unit. $\dfrac{\pi}{2}\ \text{rad}$ and $90^\circ$ are the same, but writing just 90 with no unit loses the mark. Use $\Delta\varphi = 2\pi\Delta t/T$ in radians."),
        MISTAKE("Saying a and x are in phase", r"Because $a = -\omega^2x$, they are in **antiphase** ($\pi$ apart): the minus sign shifts the acceleration curve by half a cycle."),
        QUIZ(r"At the instant when the displacement of an object in SHM is at its most positive value, the velocity-time graph is at...",
             ["its maximum positive value", "zero, and is falling", "its most negative value", "zero, and is rising"], 1,
             r"$v$ leads $x$ by $\pi/2$. When $x$ is at a peak, $v = 0$, and the $v$ curve is heading down to its negative peak, so it is falling.",
             hint="Use the dashed line at T/4 in Figure 1.",
             feedback=["That happens a quarter period earlier, when x = 0.", "", "That happens a quarter of a period later, when x = 0 again.", "The v graph is falling at this moment because the object is about to move back towards equilibrium."]),
        QUIZ(r"On a graph of acceleration (vertical) against displacement (horizontal) for SHM, the gradient is $-9.0\ \text{s}^{-2}$. What is the frequency?",
             [r"$3.0\ \text{Hz}$", r"$0.48\ \text{Hz}$", r"$9.0\ \text{Hz}$", r"$1.4\ \text{Hz}$"], 1,
             r"Gradient $= -\omega^2 = -9.0$, so $\omega = 3.0\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$ and $f = \dfrac{3.0}{2\pi} = 0.48\ \text{Hz}$.",
             hint=r"Gradient $= -\omega^2$.",
             feedback=[r"3.0 is $\omega$ in rad/s. Divide by $2\pi$.", "", r"9.0 is $\omega^2$. Take the square root, then divide by $2\pi$.", r"$1.4$ is $9.0/2\pi$: you forgot to take the square root of the gradient."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"The velocity–time graph of an oscillator is a sine curve of maximum value $0.30\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ and period $2.0\ \text{s}$. Find the amplitude of the oscillation and the maximum acceleration.",
           r"$\omega = \dfrac{2\pi}{2.0} = \pi\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$. Since $v_{max} = \omega A$, $A = \dfrac{0.30}{\pi} = 0.095\ \text{m}$. Maximum acceleration $a_{max} = \omega v_{max} = \pi \times 0.30 = 0.94\ \text{m s}^{-2}$."),
        PQ(r"Two oscillators have the same period of $1.5\ \text{s}$. One reaches its maximum displacement $0.25\ \text{s}$ later than the other. Calculate the phase difference in degrees and in radians.",
           r"$\Delta\varphi = 360^\circ \times \dfrac{0.25}{1.5} = 60^\circ$. In radians $2\pi \times \dfrac{0.25}{1.5} = \dfrac{\pi}{3} = 1.05\ \text{rad}$."),
        PQ(r"A body has displacement $x = 0.050\sin(4\pi t)$ in SI units. Calculate its displacement, velocity and acceleration at $t = 0.30\ \text{s}$. State the direction of the acceleration.",
           r"$4\pi \times 0.30 = 3.770$ rad. $x = 0.050\sin 3.770 = -0.029\ \text{m}$. $v = 4\pi \times 0.050\cos 3.770 = -0.51\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. $a = -(4\pi)^2x = -157.9 \times (-0.0294) = +4.6\ \text{m s}^{-2}$. The acceleration is positive, which is towards equilibrium because $x$ is negative."),
        PQ(r"A student plots $a$ against $x$ for a mass on a spring and finds a straight line through the origin of gradient $-45\ \text{s}^{-2}$. (a) Explain why this shows SHM. (b) Find the frequency.",
           r"(a) The line through the origin with negative gradient means $a \propto -x$: acceleration is proportional to displacement and opposite in direction, which is the definition of SHM. (b) $\omega^2 = 45$, so $\omega = 6.7\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$ and $f = \dfrac{6.7}{2\pi} = 1.07\ \text{Hz}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: graphs of x, v and a against time, and of a against x, are common in Paper 2 structured questions, and in Paper 1 you may be given a graph and asked for the period or for the point of maximum speed. Always read the period from peak to peak, not from zero to a peak."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$x$, $v$ and $a$ are sinusoidal with the same period; velocity is the gradient of $x$, acceleration the gradient of $v$.",
            r"$v$ leads $x$ by $\frac{\pi}{2}$; $a$ is in antiphase with $x$ (phase difference $\pi$).",
            r"$a$–$x$ graph: straight line through the origin, gradient $-\omega^2$. $v$–$x$ graph: an ellipse.",
            r"Phase difference $\Delta\varphi = 2\pi\dfrac{\Delta t}{T}$ (radians) or $360^\circ\dfrac{\Delta t}{T}$.",
            "Always use radian mode when you evaluate sine and cosine."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3. Pendulums and mass-spring systems
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Pendulums and mass-spring systems",
    title="The mass-spring system and the simple pendulum",
    slug="phl2-pendulum-mass-spring",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Show that a mass on a spring and a small-angle pendulum obey a = −ω²x, derive T = 2π√(m/k) and T = 2π√(L/g), and use them to find k and g.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive T = 2π√(m/k) for a horizontal and a vertical mass-spring system",
        "Derive T = 2π√(L/g) for a simple pendulum and state the small-angle condition",
        "Find k and g from experimental data using straight-line graphs",
        "Combine springs in series and parallel and predict the new period",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Restoring force", "Spring constant", "Small-angle approximation", "Simple pendulum", "Springs in series and parallel", "Period independent of mass"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In the last two lessons we studied SHM through its equation $a = -\omega^2x$. Now we meet the two systems that physics laboratories use most to produce it: a **mass on a spring** and a **simple pendulum**. For each one we ask: what is the restoring force, and does it give $a = -\omega^2x$? If it does, we can read off the period."),
        H("The mass-spring system"),
        T(r"A spring that obeys Hooke's law has a force proportional to its extension: the force it exerts is $F = -kx$, where $k$ is the **spring constant** (stiffness, in $\text{N m}^{-1}$) and the minus sign shows that the force always points back towards the unstretched position."),
        T(r"Take a mass $m$ on a smooth horizontal surface attached to such a spring. Newton's second law gives $ma = -kx$, so"),
        F(r"a = -\frac{k}{m}\,x"),
        T(r"This has the form $a = -\omega^2x$ with $\omega^2 = \dfrac{k}{m}$. So the motion is SHM and"),
        D(r"For a mass $m$ on a spring of spring constant $k$,", title="Period of a mass-spring system",
          formula=r"T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}}, \qquad f = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}}",
          note="A stiffer spring (larger k) gives a shorter period. A larger mass gives a longer period."),
        T(r"**Hanging vertically.** Weight now acts as well. At equilibrium the spring is stretched by $e$ where $ke = mg$. If the mass is pulled a further distance $x$ downwards, the net force upward is $k(e + x) - mg = kx$. So again $ma = -kx$ and the period is the same, $2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$. Gravity only shifts the equilibrium position; it does not change the period. Since $k = mg/e$, the period can also be written $T = 2\pi\sqrt{e/g}$, which is useful when the extension $e$ is given."),
        KEY("Springs in combination", r"Two springs of constants $k_1$ and $k_2$ behave like a single spring of constant $k$:", cards=[
            ["In parallel (side by side)", r"$k = k_1 + k_2$. Both springs share the extension, and their forces add. The combination is stiffer."],
            ["In series (end to end)", r"$\dfrac{1}{k} = \dfrac{1}{k_1} + \dfrac{1}{k_2}$. Each spring carries the full force but the extensions add. The combination is softer."],
        ]),
        H("The simple pendulum"),
        T(r"A **simple pendulum** is a small heavy bob of mass $m$ on a light, inextensible string of length $L$ (measured from the pivot to the centre of the bob). The bob swings along a circular arc. Let the string make a small angle $\theta$ (in radians) with the vertical. The weight $mg$ has a component $mg\sin\theta$ along the arc, directed back towards the lowest point. So"),
        F(r"ma = -mg\sin\theta"),
        T(r"The minus sign shows the force always points back to equilibrium. Now use the **small-angle approximation**: for $\theta$ in radians and small, $\sin\theta \approx \theta$. The arc length from the lowest point is $x = L\theta$, so $\theta = \dfrac{x}{L}$ and"),
        F(r"a = -\frac{g}{L}\,x"),
        T(r"Again the form $a = -\omega^2x$ appears, with $\omega^2 = \dfrac{g}{L}$:"),
        D(r"For a simple pendulum of length $L$ in a place where the gravitational field strength is $g$ (small amplitudes only),", title="Period of a simple pendulum",
          formula=r"T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}",
          note="The mass m has cancelled: the period does not depend on the mass of the bob, and for small angles it does not depend on the amplitude."),
        T(r"**How small is small?** A computer calculation of the exact period gives these ratios of the true period to the formula value: about $1.0005$ at $5^\circ$, $1.0019$ at $10^\circ$, $1.0077$ at $20^\circ$, $1.017$ at $30^\circ$ and $1.073$ at $60^\circ$. So below about $10^\circ$ the formula is accurate to better than 0.2%. At large angles the true period is longer, and the motion is no longer SHM."),
        H("Finding g and k from experiments"),
        T(r"Squaring $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$ gives $T^2 = \dfrac{4\pi^2}{g}L$. A graph of $T^2$ (vertical) against $L$ is a straight line through the origin with gradient $\dfrac{4\pi^2}{g}$, so $g = \dfrac{4\pi^2}{\text{gradient}}$. For a spring, $T^2 = \dfrac{4\pi^2}{k}m$, so a graph of $T^2$ against $m$ has gradient $\dfrac{4\pi^2}{k}$. Timing many oscillations (for example 20) and dividing reduces the effect of your reaction time."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a spring and a mass", intro=r"A mass of $0.50\ \text{kg}$ is attached to a horizontal spring of constant $80\ \text{N m}^{-1}$ and pulled $6.0\ \text{cm}$ from equilibrium, then released. Find the period, the frequency and the maximum speed.", steps=[
            S("Period", math=r"T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{m}{k}} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{0.50}{80}} = 0.497\ \text{s}"),
            S("Frequency", math=r"f = \frac{1}{T} = 2.01\ \text{Hz}"),
            S(r"Amplitude $= 0.060\ \text{m}$; angular frequency", math=r"\omega = \sqrt{\frac{k}{m}} = \sqrt{160} = 12.65\ \text{rad s}^{-1}"),
            S("Maximum speed", math=r"v_{max} = \omega A = 12.65 \times 0.060 = 0.76\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
        ], check=r"\sqrt{0.5/80} = 0.07906,\ \ 2\pi \times 0.07906 = 0.4967"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a vertical spring and its extension", intro=r"The same mass of $0.50\ \text{kg}$ hangs from the same spring, $k = 80\ \text{N m}^{-1}$, with $g = 10\ \text{m s}^{-2}$. (a) How far does the spring stretch at equilibrium? (b) Find the period of vertical oscillations using the extension.", steps=[
            S("Equilibrium: spring force equals weight", math=r"ke = mg \Rightarrow e = \frac{0.50 \times 10}{80} = 0.0625\ \text{m}"),
            S("Period from $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{e/g}$", math=r"T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{0.0625}{10}} = 0.497\ \text{s}"),
            S("Compare", text="This matches Example 1. The period does not depend on gravity once the spring and the mass are fixed."),
        ], check=r"2\pi\sqrt{0.00625} = 0.4967"),
        EX("Worked example 3: measuring g with a pendulum", intro=r"A student times $20$ complete swings of a pendulum of length $0.800\ \text{m}$ and finds $35.9\ \text{s}$. Calculate $g$.", steps=[
            S("Period", math=r"T = \frac{35.9}{20} = 1.795\ \text{s}"),
            S("Rearrange $T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}$", math=r"g = \frac{4\pi^2L}{T^2}"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"g = \frac{4\pi^2 \times 0.800}{1.795^2} = 9.80\ \text{m s}^{-2}"),
        ], check=r"39.478 \times 0.8 = 31.58,\ \ 31.58 / 3.222 = 9.802"),
        EX("Worked example 4: a pendulum clock on a mountain", intro=r"A pendulum clock is accurate at sea level. It is moved to the summit of Mount Cameroon, height $4040\ \text{m}$ above sea level. The radius of the Earth is $6.37\times 10^6\ \text{m}$. By how much does the clock lose in one day? (Ignore temperature effects.)", steps=[
            S("At height $h$ the field strength is $g\\left(\\dfrac{R}{R+h}\\right)^2$. So $g$ falls by a fraction about $\\dfrac{2h}{R}$ and the period rises by about", math=r"\frac{\Delta T}{T} \approx \frac{h}{R} = \frac{4040}{6.37\times10^{6}} = 6.3\times10^{-4}"),
            S("The clock runs slow by this fraction. Seconds in a day: $86\\,400$", math=r"6.34\times10^{-4} \times 86\,400 = 55\ \text{s}"),
            S("Conclude", text="The clock would lose about 55 seconds each day. The change is real, though small."),
        ], check=r"(R/(R+h))^{-1} - 1 = 6.34\times 10^{-4}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using the mass of the bob in the pendulum formula", r"The period of a simple pendulum does not depend on the mass. Doubling the mass leaves $T$ unchanged. In the spring formula, however, $m$ does matter."),
        MISTAKE("Measuring the length to the end of the string", r"$L$ is measured from the pivot to the **centre of the bob**, not to the top of the bob or the end of the string."),
        MISTAKE("Large swings", r"The formula $T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$ needs a small angle, below about $10^\circ$. A $60^\circ$ swing has a period about 7% longer than the formula gives."),
        MISTAKE("Adding spring constants in series", r"Springs in **series** combine like resistors in **parallel**: $\frac{1}{k} = \frac{1}{k_1} + \frac{1}{k_2}$. Springs in **parallel** add: $k = k_1 + k_2$."),
        QUIZ("The mass on a spring is changed from 0.10 kg to 0.40 kg. What happens to the period?",
             ["It doubles.", "It is multiplied by four.", "It halves.", "It stays the same."], 0,
             r"$T \propto \sqrt{m}$. Quadrupling the mass multiplies the period by $\sqrt{4} = 2$.",
             hint=r"$T = 2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$: the period depends on the square root of the mass.",
             feedback=["", "That would be true if T were proportional to m. It is proportional to the square root of m.", "A larger mass makes the oscillation slower, so the period grows.", "That is true for a pendulum, not for a mass on a spring."]),
        QUIZ(r"A pendulum clock is taken to the Moon, where $g$ is about one sixth of its value on Earth. How does the period compare?",
             [r"Unchanged", r"Six times longer", r"About 2.4 times longer", r"About 2.4 times shorter"], 2,
             r"$T \propto \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{g}}$. With $g$ divided by 6, $T$ is multiplied by $\sqrt{6} \approx 2.4$.",
             hint=r"$T = 2\pi\sqrt{L/g}$.",
             feedback=["The period depends on $g$, not on the mass of the bob.", r"The period depends on the square root of $1/g$, not on $1/g$ itself.", "", "Weaker gravity means weaker restoring force, so the pendulum swings more slowly and the period increases."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A student plots $T^2$ against $m$ for a spring and obtains a straight line of gradient $1.58\ \text{s}^2\,\text{kg}^{-1}$ through the origin. Find the spring constant.",
           r"$T^2 = \dfrac{4\pi^2}{k}m$, so the gradient is $\dfrac{4\pi^2}{k}$. $k = \dfrac{4\pi^2}{1.58} = \dfrac{39.48}{1.58} = 25\ \text{N m}^{-1}$."),
        PQ(r"What is the length of a simple pendulum whose period is $2.00\ \text{s}$ (a seconds pendulum takes one second for each swing from one side to the other)? Use $g = 9.81\ \text{m s}^{-2}$.",
           r"$L = \dfrac{gT^2}{4\pi^2} = \dfrac{9.81 \times 4.00}{39.48} = 0.994\ \text{m}$."),
        PQ(r"Two springs of constants $60\ \text{N m}^{-1}$ and $40\ \text{N m}^{-1}$ support a mass of $0.25\ \text{kg}$. Find the period of oscillation when the springs are (a) in parallel, (b) in series.",
           r"(a) $k = 60 + 40 = 100\ \text{N m}^{-1}$, $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\dfrac{0.25}{100}} = 0.314\ \text{s}$. (b) $\dfrac{1}{k} = \dfrac{1}{60} + \dfrac{1}{40} = \dfrac{5}{120}$, so $k = 24\ \text{N m}^{-1}$ and $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\dfrac{0.25}{24}} = 0.641\ \text{s}$. The series combination is softer, so its period is longer."),
        PQ(r"A bob of mass $m$ hangs on a string of length $L$ and is displaced a small horizontal distance $x$ from equilibrium. Show that the motion is SHM and write down the period.",
           r"For a small angle $\theta$ the restoring force along the arc is $-mg\sin\theta \approx -mg\theta$. With $\theta = x/L$, $ma = -\dfrac{mg}{L}x$, so $a = -\dfrac{g}{L}x$. This is of the form $a = -\omega^2x$ with $\omega^2 = \dfrac{g}{L}$, so the motion is SHM with $T = \dfrac{2\pi}{\omega} = 2\pi\sqrt{\dfrac{L}{g}}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: expect derivations (show that the motion is SHM), calculations of T from k, m, L and g, and graph questions on T squared against L or m. These experiments are also standard in the practical Paper 3, where the number of oscillations timed and the graph gradient are marked."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Mass on a spring: $a = -\dfrac{k}{m}x$, $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\dfrac{m}{k}}$; the same for vertical oscillations, where $T = 2\pi\sqrt{e/g}$.",
            r"Springs in parallel: $k = k_1 + k_2$; in series: $\dfrac{1}{k} = \dfrac{1}{k_1} + \dfrac{1}{k_2}$.",
            r"Simple pendulum, small angle: $a = -\dfrac{g}{L}x$, $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\dfrac{L}{g}}$, independent of the mass.",
            r"A graph of $T^2$ against $L$ has gradient $\dfrac{4\pi^2}{g}$; of $T^2$ against $m$, gradient $\dfrac{4\pi^2}{k}$.",
            r"Small angle means below about $10^\circ$, in radians, so that $\sin\theta \approx \theta$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 4. Energy in SHM
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Energy, damping and resonance",
    title="Energy in simple harmonic motion",
    slug="phl2-shm-energy",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Show that kinetic and potential energy trade places in SHM while the total ½mω²A² stays constant, and use energy to find speeds and displacements.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive expressions for KE, PE and total energy in SHM",
        "Sketch energy against displacement and against time",
        "Use energy conservation to find speed or displacement",
        "State how total energy depends on amplitude and frequency",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Kinetic energy", "Potential energy", "Total energy ½mω²A²", "Energy graphs", "Conservation of energy", "Energy frequency is twice f"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"When a child on a swing at a village playground reaches the highest point, she stops for an instant and her energy is all stored as gravitational potential energy. At the lowest point she is moving fastest and the energy is all kinetic. In between the energy is shared. SHM is the cleanest example of this exchange, and the sharing is described by simple formulas."),
        H("Kinetic energy"),
        T(r"The kinetic energy of an oscillating object of mass $m$ and speed $v$ is $\tfrac12mv^2$. Using $v^2 = \omega^2(A^2 - x^2)$ from the first lesson,"),
        F(r"E_k = \tfrac12 m\omega^2\left(A^2 - x^2\right)"),
        T(r"It is largest, $\tfrac12m\omega^2A^2$, at the equilibrium position $x = 0$ and zero at the end points $x = \pm A$."),
        H("Potential energy"),
        T(r"**Spring.** The restoring force is $F = -kx$. The work done stretching it from $0$ to $x$ is the area under the force–extension graph, $\tfrac12kx^2$. Since $k = m\omega^2$, the elastic potential energy is $E_p = \tfrac12kx^2 = \tfrac12m\omega^2x^2$."),
        T(r"**Pendulum.** For a small angle the bob is raised a height $h = L(1 - \cos\theta) \approx \dfrac{L\theta^2}{2} = \dfrac{x^2}{2L}$. So $E_p = mgh = \dfrac{mg}{2L}x^2 = \tfrac12m\omega^2x^2$ with $\omega^2 = \dfrac{g}{L}$. The same formula appears: this is the general result for SHM, taking zero potential energy at equilibrium."),
        F(r"E_p = \tfrac12 m\omega^2x^2"),
        H("Total energy"),
        D(r"Adding the two energies, the $x^2$ terms cancel:", title="Total energy in SHM",
          formula=r"E = E_k + E_p = \tfrac12m\omega^2A^2 = \tfrac12kA^2",
          note="Total energy is constant (no energy is lost if there is no damping). It is proportional to the amplitude squared and to the frequency squared (ω = 2πf)."),
        IMG("al-phl2-shm-energy.svg", alt="Energy against displacement from minus A to plus A. Potential energy is an upward parabola with minimum at zero. Kinetic energy is a downward parabola with maximum at zero. Total energy is a horizontal line at their common maximum. The curves cross at A over root two.", caption="Figure 1. Energy against displacement. The two parabolas always add up to the horizontal line."),
        T(r"Notice three features. The curves are **symmetrical about $x = 0$**. They cross at the point where $E_k = E_p$, which happens when $\tfrac12m\omega^2x^2 = \tfrac14m\omega^2A^2$, that is $x = \dfrac{A}{\sqrt2} = 0.71A$. And at every $x$ the vertical distance from the horizontal line down to the PE curve is the KE."),
        H("Energy against time"),
        T(r"With $x = A\sin\omega t$, $E_p = E\sin^2\omega t$ and $E_k = E\cos^2\omega t$. Both graphs are 'sine squared' curves that never go below zero. They oscillate between $0$ and $E$ with period $\dfrac{T}{2}$, so the **frequency of the energy exchange is twice the frequency of the oscillation**. Over a full cycle the average of each is $\dfrac{E}{2}$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: energy of a spring oscillator", intro=r"A mass of $0.20\ \text{kg}$ on a horizontal spring of constant $50\ \text{N m}^{-1}$ oscillates with amplitude $0.060\ \text{m}$. Find (a) the total energy, (b) the maximum speed, (c) the kinetic energy and speed when the displacement is $0.040\ \text{m}$.", steps=[
            S("Total energy", math=r"E = \tfrac12kA^2 = \tfrac12 \times 50 \times 0.060^2 = 0.090\ \text{J}"),
            S("At equilibrium all the energy is kinetic", math=r"\tfrac12mv_{max}^2 = 0.090 \Rightarrow v_{max} = \sqrt{\frac{2 \times 0.090}{0.20}} = 0.95\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Potential energy at $x = 0.040\\ \\text{m}$", math=r"E_p = \tfrac12 \times 50 \times 0.040^2 = 0.040\ \text{J}"),
            S("Kinetic energy by subtraction", math=r"E_k = 0.090 - 0.040 = 0.050\ \text{J}"),
            S("Speed", math=r"v = \sqrt{\frac{2 \times 0.050}{0.20}} = 0.71\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
        ], check=r"\omega A = \sqrt{250} \times 0.060 = 0.9487,\quad \omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2} = 0.7071"),
        EX("Worked example 2: energy and a pendulum", intro=r"A pendulum of length $1.00\ \text{m}$ is released from rest at $8^\circ$ to the vertical. Use energy to find the maximum speed of the bob, with $g = 10\ \text{m s}^{-2}$. Compare with the SHM result.", steps=[
            S("Height gained at the release point", math=r"h = L(1 - \cos 8^\circ) = 1.00 \times 0.009732 = 0.00973\ \text{m}"),
            S("Energy conservation: $\\tfrac12mv^2 = mgh$", math=r"v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2 \times 10 \times 0.00973} = 0.441\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("SHM method", math=r"A = L\theta = 1.00 \times 0.1396 = 0.1396\ \text{m},\ \ \omega = \sqrt{g/L} = 3.162 \Rightarrow v_{max} = \omega A = 0.4415\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Compare", text="The two answers differ by about 0.1%, which shows that the small-angle SHM approximation is excellent at 8 degrees."),
        ], check=r"\sqrt{0.19464} = 0.44118,\quad 3.1623 \times 0.13963 = 0.44154"),
        EX("Worked example 3: where is the energy shared in a given ratio?", intro=r"At what displacement is the kinetic energy equal to three times the potential energy?", steps=[
            S("Write the condition", math=r"E_k = 3E_p \Rightarrow E = E_k + E_p = 4E_p"),
            S("Substitute the formulas", math=r"\tfrac12m\omega^2A^2 = 4 \times \tfrac12m\omega^2x^2"),
            S("Solve", math=r"x^2 = \frac{A^2}{4} \Rightarrow x = \pm\frac{A}{2}"),
        ], check=r"E_p = \tfrac12m\omega^2\frac{A^2}{4} = \frac{E}{4},\quad E_k = \frac{3E}{4}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Thinking energy is lost as the oscillator slows down", "Near the ends the kinetic energy falls but the potential energy rises by the same amount. The total stays constant. Energy is lost only through damping (friction, air resistance)."),
        MISTAKE("Doubling the amplitude doubles the energy", r"Energy is proportional to $A^2$. Doubling the amplitude multiplies the total energy by $4$ and the maximum speed by $2$."),
        MISTAKE("Calling the frequency of the energy graph $f$", r"Kinetic energy is largest twice per oscillation (once in each direction through the centre), so its frequency is $2f$."),
        MISTAKE("Using total energy at the wrong displacement", r"The total energy $\tfrac12kA^2$ uses the **amplitude**. The potential energy $\tfrac12kx^2$ uses the **present displacement** $x$."),
        QUIZ(r"An oscillator has total energy $E$. At what displacement is its potential energy equal to its kinetic energy?",
             [r"$\dfrac{A}{2}$", r"$\dfrac{A}{\sqrt2}$", r"$\dfrac{A}{4}$", r"$\dfrac{A}{\sqrt3}$"], 1,
             r"$E_p = \tfrac12E$ gives $\tfrac12m\omega^2x^2 = \tfrac14m\omega^2A^2$, so $x^2 = \dfrac{A^2}{2}$ and $x = \dfrac{A}{\sqrt2}$.",
             hint=r"Set $E_p = \tfrac12 E$ and solve for $x$.",
             feedback=[r"At $x = A/2$ the potential energy is only $E/4$ (it depends on $x^2$).", "", r"$x = A/4$ gives $E_p = E/16$.", r"$x = A/\sqrt3$ gives $E_p = E/3$."]),
        QUIZ("The amplitude of an oscillating mass on a spring is doubled. Which statement is true?",
             ["The total energy doubles and the period doubles.", "The total energy is four times larger and the period is unchanged.", "The total energy is four times larger and the period is doubled.", "The total energy doubles and the period is unchanged."], 1,
             r"$E = \tfrac12kA^2$ is proportional to $A^2$. The period $2\pi\sqrt{m/k}$ does not depend on $A$.",
             hint="Which quantities in the formulas contain the amplitude?",
             feedback=["The period of SHM is independent of amplitude.", "", "The period does not change with amplitude.", "Energy is proportional to the square of the amplitude, so it is multiplied by 4, not 2."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A $0.30\ \text{kg}$ mass oscillates in SHM with frequency $1.5\ \text{Hz}$ and amplitude $0.040\ \text{m}$. Calculate its total energy.",
           r"$\omega = 2\pi \times 1.5 = 9.42\ \text{rad s}^{-1}$. $E = \tfrac12m\omega^2A^2 = \tfrac12 \times 0.30 \times 88.8 \times 0.0016 = 0.021\ \text{J}$."),
        PQ(r"A $0.50\ \text{kg}$ mass on a spring of constant $200\ \text{N m}^{-1}$ has amplitude $0.050\ \text{m}$. Find its speed and kinetic energy when the displacement is $0.030\ \text{m}$.",
           r"$E_k = \tfrac12k(A^2 - x^2) = 100\,(0.0025 - 0.0009) = 0.16\ \text{J}$. Then $v = \sqrt{\dfrac{2 \times 0.16}{0.50}} = 0.80\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Check with $v = \omega\sqrt{A^2 - x^2} = 20 \times 0.04 = 0.80\ \text{m s}^{-1}$."),
        PQ("Sketch graphs of kinetic energy, potential energy and total energy of an SHM oscillator against time for two periods, and state the frequency of the energy curves in terms of the frequency f of the motion.",
           r"The total energy is a horizontal line at $E = \tfrac12m\omega^2A^2$. Kinetic energy follows $E\cos^2\omega t$ (starting at $E$ if $x = A\sin\omega t$ starts at $x = 0$) and potential energy follows $E\sin^2\omega t$. They are mirror images that add up to $E$ at every time. Each has frequency $2f$ because they reach a maximum twice per oscillation."),
        PQ(r"Show that $E_k + E_p$ is constant for SHM with $x = A\sin\omega t$.",
           r"$E_k = \tfrac12mv^2 = \tfrac12m\omega^2A^2\cos^2\omega t$ and $E_p = \tfrac12m\omega^2x^2 = \tfrac12m\omega^2A^2\sin^2\omega t$. Adding gives $\tfrac12m\omega^2A^2(\cos^2\omega t + \sin^2\omega t) = \tfrac12m\omega^2A^2$, which is independent of $t$."),
        TIP("In the exam: energy graphs against displacement and against time, and 'find the speed at a given displacement using energy', are typical Paper 2 parts, and Paper 1 often asks how total energy changes with amplitude. Always say that total energy is constant only if there is no damping."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"$E_k = \tfrac12m\omega^2(A^2 - x^2)$, $E_p = \tfrac12m\omega^2x^2$ and total $E = \tfrac12m\omega^2A^2 = \tfrac12kA^2$.",
            "Energy is constant if there is no damping; it shuttles between kinetic and potential forms.",
            "Energy against displacement: two parabolas that add to a horizontal line. They cross at x = A/√2.",
            r"Energy against time: $\sin^2$ and $\cos^2$ curves with frequency $2f$.",
            r"Total energy is proportional to $A^2$ and to $f^2$; the period does not depend on $A$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5. Damping and resonance
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Energy, damping and resonance",
    title="Damping, forced oscillations and resonance",
    slug="phl2-damping-resonance",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Describe light, critical and heavy damping, forced oscillations, the amplitude–frequency curve and the phase lag, and explain resonance with real examples.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish free, damped and forced oscillations",
        "Describe light, critical and heavy damping and give uses of each",
        "Explain resonance and sketch amplitude against driving frequency for different damping",
        "Apply the idea of a constant fractional decay of amplitude per cycle",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Free oscillation", "Damping", "Critical damping", "Forced oscillation", "Natural frequency", "Resonance", "Phase lag"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In real life, oscillations do not go on for ever. A swing slows down if nobody pushes it. The needle of a moving-coil meter in the laboratory settles quickly on a reading. A vehicle on the dirt road to Kumbo shakes violently at one particular speed and more gently at others. All three situations are about **damping** and **resonance**, the two extra ideas that complete the study of oscillations."),
        H("Free and damped oscillations"),
        D(r"A **free oscillation** is one in which a system, once displaced and released, oscillates with no external force other than its own restoring force. It does so at its **natural frequency** $f_0$. In a perfect free oscillation the amplitude would stay constant.", title="Free oscillation and natural frequency"),
        D(r"**Damping** is the loss of energy of an oscillating system to its surroundings, caused by dissipative forces such as friction and air or liquid resistance. The amplitude of a damped oscillation decreases with time.", title="Damping"),
        T(r"For **light damping** the oscillator still swings back and forth, with each swing smaller than the one before. A useful fact: for light damping the amplitude falls by the **same fraction in each cycle**. If the amplitude falls to $0.80$ of its value each cycle (an exponential decay), it takes the values $10.0,\ 8.0,\ 6.4,\ 5.1,\ \dots\ \text{cm}$. The period is almost unchanged by light damping."),
        KEY("Three degrees of damping", "Imagine pulling a mass down in oil, in water and in air.", cards=[
            ["Light damping", "The system oscillates many times with slowly decaying amplitude. Example: a playground swing, a pendulum clock without its driving mechanism."],
            ["Critical damping", "The system returns to equilibrium in the shortest possible time **without** overshooting and oscillating. Example: car shock absorbers, a moving-coil meter, a door closer."],
            ["Heavy damping (overdamping)", "The system returns very slowly to equilibrium without oscillating. Example: a door closer set too stiffly, a mass in thick oil."],
        ]),
        H("Forced oscillations and resonance"),
        D(r"A **forced oscillation** is one in which a periodic external force (the **driver**) acts on an oscillating system, making it oscillate with the driving frequency $f$, not its natural frequency.", title="Forced oscillation"),
        T(r"Push a swing repeatedly. At first the swing moves a bit confusedly (the 'transient'), but after a short time it settles into a steady oscillation at the **frequency of your pushes**, not at its own natural frequency. How large the steady amplitude is depends on how close the driving frequency is to $f_0$."),
        D(r"**Resonance** is the large-amplitude response of an oscillating system when the driving frequency is equal (or very close) to its natural frequency $f_0$. At resonance the driver transfers energy to the system most efficiently.", title="Resonance"),
        IMG("al-phl2-resonance-curves.svg", alt="Graph of amplitude against driving frequency for three levels of damping. The lightly damped curve has a tall narrow peak at the natural frequency f0. The medium damped curve has a lower, broader peak slightly below f0. The heavily damped curve is low and has no sharp peak.", caption="Figure 1. Amplitude against driving frequency. More damping lowers and broadens the peak, and moves it slightly towards lower frequency."),
        T(r"Read the graph. At very low driving frequency the system just follows the driver with about the same amplitude as the driver's push. At $f = f_0$ the amplitude is large. At high frequency the system cannot keep up and the amplitude is small. The **lighter** the damping, the **taller and sharper** the peak. With very heavy damping there is no clear resonance."),
        KEY("Phase of the response", r"The displacement of the system compared with the driving force:", cards=[
            [r"$f \ll f_0$", "The system moves in phase with the driver (phase difference about 0)."],
            [r"$f = f_0$", r"The displacement lags the driver by $\dfrac{\pi}{2}$ (a quarter of a cycle)."],
            [r"$f \gg f_0$", r"The displacement is in antiphase with the driver (lag of about $\pi$)."],
        ]),
        T(r"**Resonance in the real world.** Useful: a radio tuner is an electrical resonance (you will meet it in Upper Sixth), the vibration of a guitar body that boosts a note, and musical instruments such as a balafon with its gourds under each bar. **Harmful:** a bridge or building driven by wind or marching feet, a vehicle's wheels hopping on a corrugated road, a loose panel buzzing at one engine speed. Engineers add damping, change stiffness or mass, or avoid the frequency to prevent damage."),
        EX("Worked example 1: constant fractional decay", intro=r"A lightly damped oscillator starts with amplitude $10.0\ \text{cm}$. The amplitude falls to $80\%$ of its previous value in every cycle. Find (a) the amplitude after 5 cycles, (b) the fraction of energy lost in one cycle, (c) the number of cycles for the amplitude to halve.", steps=[
            S("Amplitude after $n$ cycles", math=r"A_n = 10.0 \times 0.80^n"),
            S("After 5 cycles", math=r"A_5 = 10.0 \times 0.80^5 = 3.3\ \text{cm}"),
            S("Energy is proportional to amplitude squared, so the energy after one cycle is $0.80^2$ of the original", math=r"0.80^2 = 0.64 \Rightarrow \text{fraction lost} = 1 - 0.64 = 0.36 = 36\%"),
            S("Halving: solve $0.80^n = 0.5$", math=r"n = \frac{\ln 0.5}{\ln 0.8} = 3.1\ \text{cycles}"),
        ], check=r"0.8^5 = 0.32768,\ \ 0.8^{3.106} = 0.500"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a vehicle on a corrugated road", intro=r"The ridges on a corrugated dirt road ('tôle ondulée') are $0.70\ \text{m}$ apart. Suppose (teaching value, not a measured one) the wheel and spring system of a pick-up truck has a natural frequency of $10\ \text{Hz}$. At what speed does the truck shake the most?", steps=[
            S("Each ridge gives the wheel a push. The driving frequency is the number of ridges met each second", math=r"f = \frac{v}{0.70}"),
            S("Resonance needs the driving frequency to equal the natural frequency", math=r"\frac{v}{0.70} = 10 \Rightarrow v = 7.0\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Convert to kilometres per hour", math=r"7.0 \times 3.6 = 25\ \text{km h}^{-1}"),
            S("Comment", text="Drivers find that going much faster or much slower reduces the shaking. At 14 m/s the driving frequency is 20 Hz, far above resonance, and the vibration is smaller."),
        ], check=r"v = f\lambda = 10 \times 0.70 = 7.0"),
        EX("Worked example 3: frequency and phase for a driven mass on a spring", intro=r"A mass of $0.10\ \text{kg}$ on a spring of constant $40\ \text{N m}^{-1}$ is driven by a small motor. Find the natural frequency and describe the response when the motor runs at $1.0\ \text{Hz}$, $3.2\ \text{Hz}$ and $8.0\ \text{Hz}$.", steps=[
            S("Natural frequency", math=r"f_0 = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{\frac{k}{m}} = \frac{1}{2\pi}\sqrt{400} = 3.18\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("At $1.0\\ \\text{Hz}$ (below $f_0$)", text="The amplitude is small and the mass moves almost in phase with the driver."),
            S("At $3.2\\ \\text{Hz}$ (essentially $f_0$)", text="The amplitude is largest (resonance) and the mass lags the driver by about a quarter of a cycle."),
            S("At $8.0\\ \\text{Hz}$ (well above $f_0$)", text="The amplitude is small and the mass moves almost in antiphase with the driver. In all three cases the mass oscillates at the driving frequency."),
        ], check=r"\sqrt{40/0.10} = 20,\ \ 20/2\pi = 3.183"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up natural and driving frequency", r"The **natural** frequency belongs to the system (set by $k$ and $m$, or $L$ and $g$). The **driving** frequency belongs to the driver. In a steady forced oscillation the system vibrates at the driving frequency."),
        MISTAKE("Saying critical damping means no damping", "Critical damping is the amount of damping that brings the system to rest in the shortest time without overshoot. It is a lot of damping, but not too much."),
        MISTAKE("Saying resonance needs a big driving force", "Resonance needs the right frequency. Even a small force repeated at the natural frequency can build a very large amplitude, because energy builds up cycle after cycle."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting energy in resonance", "At resonance the energy supplied by the driver in each cycle equals the energy lost to damping. The amplitude stops growing when these two are equal."),
        QUIZ("A car's shock absorbers are designed to give critical damping. Why?",
             ["So the car oscillates for a long time after hitting a bump.", "So the car returns to its normal position quickly without bouncing.", "So the suspension has no spring at all.", "So the amplitude of the wheel vibration is as large as possible."], 1,
             "Critical damping returns the system to equilibrium in the shortest time without overshoot, which is what you want after a bump.",
             hint="Think of how a good car behaves after a pothole.",
             feedback=["That is light damping, which would make the car bounce many times.", "", "The spring is still needed to support the car. The damper only removes energy.", "Large amplitude is what dampers are designed to prevent."]),
        QUIZ("A mass-spring system has natural frequency 4.0 Hz and is driven at 4.0 Hz. If the damping is increased, what happens to the peak amplitude at resonance?",
             ["It increases.", "It stays the same.", "It decreases.", "It becomes infinite."], 2,
             "More damping removes more energy per cycle, so the amplitude at which energy in equals energy out is smaller. The peak is lower and broader.",
             hint="Look at the three curves in Figure 1.",
             feedback=["Lighter damping, not heavier, gives a taller peak.", "The peak height depends strongly on the damping.", "", "Only a system with no damping at all would have infinite amplitude, and no real system has zero damping."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ("Describe how Barton's pendulums can be used to show resonance. A set of light pendulums of different lengths hangs from one string. A heavy driver pendulum of the same length as one of them is set swinging.",
           r"The driver pendulum swings and makes the string vibrate at its own frequency, giving each light pendulum a small periodic push. Each light pendulum is a forced oscillator and swings at the driver's frequency. The light pendulum whose length equals the driver's has the same natural frequency as the driver, so it resonates and swings with a large amplitude, lagging the driver by about a quarter of a cycle. The others swing with small amplitudes."),
        PQ(r"A lightly damped pendulum has amplitude $12.0\ \text{cm}$. Its amplitude falls by $5\%$ in each swing cycle. Find the amplitude after $10$ cycles and the percentage of its energy lost in the first cycle.",
           r"Each cycle multiplies the amplitude by $0.95$. After 10 cycles, $12.0 \times 0.95^{10} = 12.0 \times 0.599 = 7.2\ \text{cm}$. Energy is proportional to amplitude squared, so the energy after one cycle is $0.95^2 = 0.9025$ of the original. Loss $= 1 - 0.9025 = 0.0975 \approx 9.8\%$."),
        PQ(r"A footbridge has a natural vibration frequency of $1.8\ \text{Hz}$. Marching soldiers cross it, taking $1.8$ steps per second. Explain why soldiers are told to break step when they cross a bridge.",
           r"Marching at $1.8$ steps per second applies a periodic force at the bridge's natural frequency. This is resonance: energy is transferred efficiently each cycle, and the amplitude of the bridge's vibration can grow large enough to damage it. By breaking step, the soldiers make the forcing irregular, so no single driving frequency matches the natural frequency and the amplitude stays small."),
        PQ("Sketch two amplitude-frequency curves for the same system, one with light damping and one with heavy damping. State two differences between the curves.",
           r"Both curves have their peak near the natural frequency $f_0$ and both approach the same small amplitude at very low frequency. Differences: (1) the peak amplitude for light damping is much higher; (2) the lightly damped peak is narrower (sharper), while the heavily damped peak is broad and low. With very heavy damping the peak moves to a slightly lower frequency than $f_0$ and may disappear."),
        TIP("In the exam: questions often ask you to describe an experiment showing resonance (Barton's pendulums or a driven spring), to sketch amplitude against driving frequency for two damping levels, and to give examples of useful and harmful resonance. Name the frequencies precisely: natural and driving."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("A free oscillation happens at the natural frequency; damping removes energy and reduces the amplitude. Light damping: amplitude falls by the same fraction each cycle.",
            "Critical damping: fastest return to equilibrium with no overshoot. Heavy damping: slow return, no oscillation.",
            "A forced oscillation takes place at the driving frequency. Resonance: large amplitude when the driving frequency matches the natural frequency.",
            "Lighter damping gives a taller, sharper resonance peak. The phase lag of the response is about 0 below resonance, a quarter cycle at resonance, and about half a cycle above.",
            "Resonance can be useful (instruments, tuning) or harmful (bridges, vibrating vehicles); engineers control it with damping or by changing the natural frequency."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6. Progressive waves
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Progressive waves",
    title="Progressive waves: equation, phase and intensity",
    slug="phl2-progressive-waves",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Describe a progressive wave with y = A sin(ωt − kx), use v = fλ and phase difference 2πΔx/λ, tell particle motion from wave motion, and link intensity to amplitude.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain what a progressive wave transfers and distinguish transverse from longitudinal waves",
        "Use v = fλ, ω = 2πf and k = 2π/λ and read a wave graph of y against x or against t",
        "Find the phase difference between two points on a wave",
        "Use intensity ∝ amplitude² and the inverse-square law for a point source",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Progressive wave", "Wavelength and period", "Wave equation y = A sin(ωt − kx)", "Phase difference", "Intensity", "Transverse and longitudinal"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A fisherman at Limbe watches a wave roll in. The water goes up and down but the boat stays near the same place while the wave moves on. What travels is the **disturbance** and its energy, not the water. This is the key idea of a **progressive (travelling) wave**: it transfers energy from one place to another without transferring matter."),
        H("Basic quantities"),
        KEY("Describing a wave", "A wave is a regular disturbance spreading through a medium (or through space for light).", cards=[
            ["Transverse wave", "Particles of the medium oscillate at right angles to the direction the wave travels. Examples: waves on a rope, light, water ripples."],
            ["Longitudinal wave", "Particles oscillate parallel to the direction of travel, making compressions and rarefactions. Examples: sound in air, a pulse along a slinky spring."],
            ["Wavelength λ", "The distance between two successive points in the same state of motion (for example crest to crest)."],
            ["Period T and frequency f", r"$T$ is the time for one complete oscillation at a point. $f = 1/T$."],
        ]),
        D(r"In one period $T$ the wave moves forward exactly one wavelength $\lambda$. So the wave speed is $v = \dfrac{\lambda}{T}$, that is", title="The wave equation (speed)", formula=r"v = f\lambda"),
        H("Two graphs of the same wave"),
        IMG("al-phl2-wave-graphs.svg", alt="Two sine graphs. The top graph shows displacement against distance at one instant, with the wavelength marked between two crests. The lower graph shows displacement against time at one point, with the period marked between two crests.", caption="Figure 1. The top graph is a snapshot and gives the wavelength. The lower graph follows one point and gives the period."),
        T(r"Examiners test whether you can tell the two graphs apart. The **displacement–distance** graph is a snapshot of the whole wave at one instant, and the distance between crests is the **wavelength**. The **displacement–time** graph follows one point on the wave, and the time between peaks is the **period**. The two look the same, so always read the label on the horizontal axis."),
        H("The equation of a progressive wave"),
        T(r"Suppose the source at $x = 0$ moves with $y = A\sin\omega t$. A point at distance $x$ along the wave repeats this motion after a delay $t' = \dfrac{x}{v}$, so"),
        F(r"y = A\sin\omega\left(t - \frac{x}{v}\right) = A\sin(\omega t - kx), \qquad k = \frac{\omega}{v} = \frac{2\pi}{\lambda}"),
        T(r"Here $k$ is the **wavenumber** (radians per metre). The wave moves in the $+x$ direction; for a wave moving in the $-x$ direction replace $-kx$ by $+kx$. Because the delay grows with $x$, points farther from the source lag behind in phase."),
        KEY("Wave speed and particle speed are different", r"The wave moves along at constant speed $v = f\lambda$ in uniform conditions. Each particle of the medium oscillates in SHM, with a speed that changes from $0$ to $\omega A$ (maximum). Do not mix the two speeds.", cards=[]),
        D(r"Two points on a wave a distance $\Delta x$ apart have a phase difference", title="Phase difference along a wave", formula=r"\Delta\varphi = \frac{2\pi\,\Delta x}{\lambda} = k\,\Delta x",
          note=r"Points one wavelength apart are in phase ($2\pi$). Points half a wavelength apart are in antiphase ($\pi$)."),
        H("Energy and intensity"),
        T(r"**Intensity** $I$ is the power (energy per second) passing perpendicularly through unit area: $I = \dfrac{P}{A}$, in $\text{W m}^{-2}$. The energy of each oscillating particle is proportional to the amplitude squared, so"),
        F(r"I \propto A^2 \qquad (\text{and also } I \propto f^2 \text{ for the same medium})"),
        T(r"For a small source sending power $P$ equally in all directions, the energy is spread over a sphere of area $4\pi r^2$, so $I = \dfrac{P}{4\pi r^2}$. This is the **inverse-square law**: doubling the distance reduces the intensity to one quarter, and so the amplitude is halved. (We assume the medium does not absorb energy.)"),
        T(r"**Speed on a stretched string.** For a string of mass per unit length $\mu$ under tension $F_T$ the wave speed is $v = \sqrt{\dfrac{F_T}{\mu}}$. A tighter or lighter string gives faster waves. Textbooks derive this result; you can use it to find the frequencies of stationary waves in the next lesson."),
        EX("Worked example 1: reading the wave equation", intro=r"A wave on a rope is described in SI units by $y = 0.020\sin\left[2\pi(5.0\,t - 2.0\,x)\right]$. Find the amplitude, frequency, wavelength, wave speed, the maximum speed of a rope particle and the displacement at $x = 0.10\ \text{m}$ and $t = 0.050\ \text{s}$.", steps=[
            S("Compare with $y = A\\sin(2\\pi f t - 2\\pi x/\\lambda)$", text="Amplitude $A = 0.020\\ \\text{m}$; $f = 5.0\\ \\text{Hz}$; $\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda} = 2.0\\ \\text{m}^{-1}$, so $\\lambda = 0.50\\ \\text{m}$."),
            S("Wave speed", math=r"v = f\lambda = 5.0 \times 0.50 = 2.5\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Maximum particle speed", math=r"v_{max} = \omega A = 2\pi \times 5.0 \times 0.020 = 0.63\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Displacement (angle in radians)", math=r"y = 0.020\sin\left[2\pi(0.25 - 0.20)\right] = 0.020\sin 0.314 = 6.2\times10^{-3}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Comment", text="The wave moves at 2.5 m/s but the rope's particles never move faster than 0.63 m/s, and they move at right angles to the wave."),
        ], check=r"\omega A = 31.416 \times 0.02 = 0.6283,\quad \sin 0.31416 = 0.30902"),
        EX("Worked example 2: phase difference", intro=r"A wave of wavelength $0.60\ \text{m}$ travels along a spring. Find the phase difference between two points $0.15\ \text{m}$ apart, and between two points $0.90\ \text{m}$ apart.", steps=[
            S("First pair", math=r"\Delta\varphi = \frac{2\pi \times 0.15}{0.60} = \frac{\pi}{2}\ \text{rad}\ (90^\circ)"),
            S("Second pair", math=r"\Delta\varphi = \frac{2\pi \times 0.90}{0.60} = 3\pi\ \text{rad}"),
            S("Simplify", text=r"$3\pi$ is one and a half cycles, which is the same as $\pi$: the two points are in antiphase (they move in opposite directions at the same time)."),
        ], check=r"0.90/0.60 = 1.5 \text{ wavelengths}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: intensity from a loudspeaker", intro=r"A small loudspeaker radiates $2.0\ \text{W}$ of sound power equally in all directions. Find the intensity at $5.0\ \text{m}$ and at $10\ \text{m}$ (ignore reflections and absorption).", steps=[
            S("Use $I = P/4\\pi r^2$ at $5.0\\ \\text{m}$", math=r"I = \frac{2.0}{4\pi \times 25} = 6.4\times10^{-3}\ \text{W m}^{-2}"),
            S("At $10\\ \\text{m}$", math=r"I = \frac{2.0}{4\pi \times 100} = 1.6\times10^{-3}\ \text{W m}^{-2}"),
            S("Compare", text="Doubling the distance gives one quarter of the intensity, so the amplitude of the sound wave halves."),
        ], check=r"6.366\times10^{-3}/4 = 1.592\times10^{-3}"),
        EX("Worked example 4: wave speed on a string", intro=r"A string of mass per unit length $5.0\ \text{g m}^{-1}$ is under a tension of $20\ \text{N}$. Find the speed of a wave on it, and the wavelength of a $200\ \text{Hz}$ wave.", steps=[
            S("Convert to SI", text="$\\mu = 0.0050\\ \\text{kg m}^{-1}$."),
            S("Speed", math=r"v = \sqrt{\frac{F_T}{\mu}} = \sqrt{\frac{20}{0.0050}} = 63\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Wavelength", math=r"\lambda = \frac{v}{f} = \frac{63.2}{200} = 0.32\ \text{m}"),
        ], check=r"\sqrt{4000} = 63.25"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Reading the period from a snapshot graph", "A displacement–distance graph gives the wavelength, not the period. You need the speed or the frequency to get the period."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up wave speed and particle speed", r"$v = f\lambda$ is the speed of the **wave**. The speed of a **particle** in the medium is $\omega\sqrt{A^2 - y^2}$ and is not the same."),
        MISTAKE("Using centimetres in the wave equation", "Convert wavelength, amplitude and distances to metres before using SI formulas."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking medium travels with the wave", "In a progressive wave the particles only oscillate about fixed positions. The energy moves, not the matter."),
        QUIZ(r"A transverse wave has a wavelength of $0.80\ \text{m}$ and a frequency of $50\ \text{Hz}$. At what speed does it travel?",
             [r"$0.016\ \text{m s}^{-1}$", r"$40\ \text{m s}^{-1}$", r"$62.5\ \text{m s}^{-1}$", r"$51\ \text{m s}^{-1}$"], 1,
             r"$v = f\lambda = 50 \times 0.80 = 40\ \text{m s}^{-1}$.",
             hint=r"Use $v = f\lambda$.",
             feedback=[r"That is $\lambda/f$. The wave speed is the product $f\lambda$.", "", r"That is $f/\lambda$. Frequency divided by wavelength is not a speed.", "You added the frequency and the wavelength. The wave speed is their product."]),
        QUIZ("The sound intensity from a small source is 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ W/m² at a distance of 3.0 m. What is the intensity at 6.0 m, assuming no absorption?",
             ["4.0 × 10⁻⁶ W/m²", "2.0 × 10⁻⁶ W/m²", "1.0 × 10⁻⁶ W/m²", "1.6 × 10⁻⁵ W/m²"], 1,
             "Inverse-square law: doubling the distance divides the intensity by 4: 8.0 × 10⁻⁶ ÷ 4 = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ W/m².",
             hint="Intensity is proportional to 1 over r squared.",
             feedback=["That would be an inverse (not inverse-square) law.", "", "That divides by 8, which would need a distance 2.8 times as large.", "That increases the intensity, but moving away from a source reduces it."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A rope wave has frequency $4.0\ \text{Hz}$ and wavelength $0.75\ \text{m}$. Find its speed and how far the wave moves in $2.5\ \text{s}$.",
           r"$v = f\lambda = 4.0 \times 0.75 = 3.0\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Distance $= vt = 3.0 \times 2.5 = 7.5\ \text{m}$."),
        PQ(r"A $25\ \text{Hz}$ wave travels at $40\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Find the phase difference between two points $0.50\ \text{m}$ apart along the wave.",
           r"$\lambda = \dfrac{v}{f} = \dfrac{40}{25} = 1.6\ \text{m}$. $\Delta\varphi = \dfrac{2\pi \times 0.50}{1.6} = 1.96\ \text{rad}$ ($112.5^\circ$)."),
        PQ(r"The amplitude of a sound wave doubles while the frequency stays the same. What happens to the intensity? What happens if the distance from a point source is doubled?",
           r"$I \propto A^2$, so doubling the amplitude multiplies the intensity by $4$. If the distance from a point source is doubled, $I \propto \dfrac{1}{r^2}$ falls to $\dfrac{1}{4}$ and the amplitude falls to $\dfrac{1}{2}$."),
        PQ("Explain, with a diagram or a description, the difference between a transverse and a longitudinal wave, and give one example of each. State what a progressive wave transfers.",
           r"In a transverse wave particles oscillate at right angles to the direction of travel (for example waves on a rope, light). In a longitudinal wave they oscillate parallel to the direction of travel, producing compressions and rarefactions (for example sound in air). A progressive wave transfers energy (and information) from place to place without transferring matter."),
        TIP("In the exam: progressive waves appear in Paper 1 (reading graphs, v = fλ, phase difference) and in Paper 2 structured questions. Underline whether the graph has distance or time on the axis before answering."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"A progressive wave transfers energy, not matter. $v = f\lambda$ and $v = \dfrac{\omega}{k}$.",
            r"$y = A\sin(\omega t - kx)$ describes a wave in the $+x$ direction, with $k = \dfrac{2\pi}{\lambda}$.",
            r"Phase difference between two points on the wave: $\Delta\varphi = \dfrac{2\pi\,\Delta x}{\lambda}$.",
            r"Intensity $\propto A^2$; for a point source $I = \dfrac{P}{4\pi r^2}$ (inverse-square law).",
            r"String wave speed $v = \sqrt{F_T/\mu}$. Displacement–distance graphs give $\lambda$; displacement–time graphs give $T$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7. Stationary waves
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="Stationary waves",
    title="Stationary waves on strings and in pipes",
    slug="phl2-stationary-waves",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Explain how stationary waves form by superposition, locate nodes and antinodes, find the harmonics of strings and pipes, and measure the speed of sound in a resonance tube.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain how a stationary wave forms and describe nodes and antinodes",
        "Compare a stationary wave with a progressive wave",
        "Find the harmonic frequencies of a string and of open and closed pipes",
        "Use the resonance-tube experiment to find the speed of sound and the end correction",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Superposition", "Node and antinode", "Harmonics", "Fundamental frequency", "Closed and open pipes", "End correction"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A guitar string, the pipe of a flute and the tube of a balafon resonator all make notes using **stationary (standing) waves**. A stationary wave does not travel along. It stays in place, with some points that never move and others that vibrate strongly. Musical pitch, tuning and the sound of every instrument depend on this idea."),
        H("How a stationary wave forms"),
        T(r"When a progressive wave is reflected at a boundary, the reflected wave travels back through the incoming wave. The two waves have the same frequency and (nearly) the same amplitude, and they **superpose**: the displacements add at every point. By the principle of superposition, the resultant displacement is the sum of the two displacements."),
        T(r"Take two waves of equal amplitude $A$ travelling in opposite directions, $y_1 = A\sin(\omega t - kx)$ and $y_2 = A\sin(\omega t + kx)$. Using $\sin a + \sin b = 2\sin\frac{a + b}{2}\cos\frac{a - b}{2}$,"),
        F(r"y = y_1 + y_2 = 2A\cos kx\,\sin\omega t"),
        T(r"Look at what this says. At every point the motion is SHM in time ($\sin\omega t$), but the **amplitude** $2A\cos kx$ depends on position. It is zero where $\cos kx = 0$ and largest (equal to $2A$) where $\cos kx = \pm 1$. Because the factor $\sin\omega t$ does not contain $x$, the pattern does not move along: it is stationary."),
        KEY("Nodes and antinodes", r"These are the two special kinds of point on a stationary wave.", cards=[
            ["Node (N)", r"A point of **zero** amplitude. The medium never moves there. Adjacent nodes are $\dfrac{\lambda}{2}$ apart."],
            ["Antinode (A)", r"A point of **maximum** amplitude. It lies halfway between two nodes. Adjacent antinodes are $\dfrac{\lambda}{2}$ apart, and a node and the next antinode are $\dfrac{\lambda}{4}$ apart."],
        ]),
        CODE("""Feature                  Progressive wave            Stationary wave
Amplitude                same at every point         varies from 0 (node) to max (antinode)
Phase                    changes along the wave      same between adjacent nodes; opposite in
                                                     neighbouring loops
Energy                   transferred along the wave  not transferred; stored in the loops
Wavelength               crest to crest              twice the distance between adjacent nodes""", "text", title="Progressive and stationary waves compared"),
        H("Stationary waves on a string"),
        T(r"A string of length $L$ fixed at both ends must have a **node at each end**, since the ends cannot move. Only certain patterns fit, those in which $L$ equals a whole number of half-wavelengths: $L = n\dfrac{\lambda}{2}$. The allowed wavelengths are $\lambda = \dfrac{2L}{n}$ and the allowed frequencies are"),
        D(r"For a string of length $L$ fixed at both ends, where $v$ is the speed of waves on the string and $n = 1, 2, 3, \dots$,", title="Harmonics on a string", formula=r"f_n = \frac{nv}{2L} = nf_1, \qquad f_1 = \frac{1}{2L}\sqrt{\frac{F_T}{\mu}}",
          note="n = 1 is the fundamental (first harmonic). All whole-number multiples are present."),
        IMG("al-phl2-string-harmonics.svg", alt="Three sketches of a string fixed at both ends. First harmonic: one loop, length a half wavelength. Second harmonic: two loops, length one wavelength. Third harmonic: three loops. Nodes are dots at the ends and between loops.", caption="Figure 1. The first three harmonics of a string fixed at both ends."),
        T(r"This explains the effects a guitarist uses: a shorter string (pressing a fret) raises $f_1$; a tighter string raises $v$ and so $f_1$; a thicker (larger $\mu$) string lowers $f_1$."),
        H("Stationary waves in pipes"),
        T(r"Sound in a pipe is a longitudinal wave. At a **closed** end the air cannot move, so there is a **displacement node**. At an **open** end the air moves freely, so there is a **displacement antinode**."),
        IMG("al-phl2-pipe-modes.svg", alt="Three sketches of a pipe closed at the left end and open at the right. Fundamental: a quarter of a wavelength fits in the pipe. Next mode: three quarters of a wavelength. Next: five quarters. Every mode has a node at the closed end and an antinode at the open end.", caption="Figure 2. A pipe closed at one end: node at the closed end, antinode at the open end."),
        KEY("Allowed frequencies in pipes", r"Let the length of the pipe be $L$ and the speed of sound $v$.", cards=[
            ["Pipe closed at one end", r"$L = (2n - 1)\dfrac{\lambda}{4}$, so $f = (2n - 1)\dfrac{v}{4L}$ for $n = 1, 2, 3, \dots$. Only **odd** harmonics: $f_1,\ 3f_1,\ 5f_1, \dots$"],
            ["Pipe open at both ends", r"Antinodes at both ends: $L = n\dfrac{\lambda}{2}$, so $f = n\dfrac{v}{2L}$. **All** harmonics are present: $f_1,\ 2f_1,\ 3f_1, \dots$. For the same length the fundamental is twice as high as that of a closed pipe."],
        ]),
        T(r"**End correction.** The antinode at an open end is actually a little beyond the end of the pipe, by a distance $e$ (the **end correction**, about $0.6$ times the pipe radius). So the effective length of the air column is $L + e$."),
        H("Measuring the speed of sound with a resonance tube"),
        T(r"A tuning fork of known frequency $f$ is held over a tube dipped in water. The water level is raised or lowered to change the air column length. A loud sound (resonance) is heard when the column length $L_1$ gives a quarter wavelength: $L_1 + e = \dfrac{\lambda}{4}$. The next resonance is at $L_2 + e = \dfrac{3\lambda}{4}$. Subtracting, the end correction cancels:"),
        F(r"L_2 - L_1 = \frac{\lambda}{2} \quad\Rightarrow\quad v = f\lambda = 2f\,(L_2 - L_1)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: a string", intro=r"A string $0.65\ \text{m}$ long is fixed at both ends, and waves travel on it at $260\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Find the wavelength and frequency of the fundamental and the frequencies of the second and third harmonics. What happens to $f_1$ if the tension is quadrupled?", steps=[
            S("Fundamental wavelength: $L = \\lambda/2$", math=r"\lambda = 2L = 1.30\ \text{m}"),
            S("Fundamental frequency", math=r"f_1 = \frac{v}{2L} = \frac{260}{1.30} = 200\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Higher harmonics", math=r"f_2 = 400\ \text{Hz},\quad f_3 = 600\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Tension quadrupled: $v = \\sqrt{F_T/\\mu}$ doubles, so $f_1$ doubles", math=r"f_1' = 400\ \text{Hz}"),
        ], check=r"260/1.3 = 200"),
        EX("Worked example 2: closed and open pipes", intro=r"A pipe $0.30\ \text{m}$ long contains air in which the speed of sound is $340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Find the three lowest resonant frequencies if the pipe is (a) closed at one end and (b) open at both ends. Ignore end correction.", steps=[
            S("(a) Closed: odd harmonics only", math=r"f_1 = \frac{v}{4L} = \frac{340}{1.2} = 283\ \text{Hz},\quad f_3 = 850\ \text{Hz},\quad f_5 = 1417\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("(b) Open: all harmonics", math=r"f_1 = \frac{v}{2L} = \frac{340}{0.60} = 567\ \text{Hz},\quad f_2 = 1133\ \text{Hz},\quad f_3 = 1700\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Compare", text="The open pipe has the higher fundamental (twice that of the closed one) and richer set of harmonics."),
        ], check=r"340/1.2 = 283.3,\quad 340/0.6 = 566.7"),
        EX("Worked example 3: resonance tube experiment", intro=r"A tuning fork of frequency $500\ \text{Hz}$ gives resonance with air column lengths $0.158\ \text{m}$ and $0.498\ \text{m}$ in a tube closed at one end. Find the speed of sound and the end correction.", steps=[
            S("Half a wavelength between the two resonances", math=r"\frac{\lambda}{2} = 0.498 - 0.158 = 0.340\ \text{m} \Rightarrow \lambda = 0.680\ \text{m}"),
            S("Speed of sound", math=r"v = f\lambda = 500 \times 0.680 = 340\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("End correction from $L_1 + e = \\lambda/4$", math=r"e = 0.170 - 0.158 = 0.012\ \text{m}"),
            S("Check with the second resonance", math=r"L_2 + e = 0.498 + 0.012 = 0.510 = \frac{3\lambda}{4}"),
        ], check=r"3 \times 0.680/4 = 0.510"),
        EX("Worked example 4: reading a stationary wave equation", intro=r"A stationary wave on a string is described in SI units by $y = 0.010\cos(5\pi x)\sin(200\pi t)$. Find the wavelength, frequency, wave speed of the component waves, the positions of the first two nodes and the amplitude at an antinode.", steps=[
            S("Compare $\\cos kx$ with $\\cos 5\\pi x$", math=r"k = 5\pi \Rightarrow \lambda = \frac{2\pi}{5\pi} = 0.40\ \text{m}"),
            S("Frequency from $\\sin\\omega t$", math=r"\omega = 200\pi \Rightarrow f = 100\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Wave speed of each travelling wave", math=r"v = f\lambda = 100 \times 0.40 = 40\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Nodes where $\\cos 5\\pi x = 0$: $5\\pi x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\ \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$", math=r"x = 0.10\ \text{m},\ 0.30\ \text{m}\quad(\text{spacing } \lambda/2 = 0.20\ \text{m})"),
            S("Antinode amplitude", text="The amplitude at an antinode is 0.010 m, which is 2A for the two component waves (each has amplitude 0.0050 m)."),
        ], check=r"200\pi/5\pi = 40"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using all harmonics for a closed pipe", r"A pipe closed at one end has only the odd harmonics, $f_1, 3f_1, 5f_1, \dots$. There is no $2f_1$ because a node at one end and an antinode at the other cannot fit a half-integer pattern."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing nodes and antinodes in a pipe", "In a pipe, an open end is an antinode of displacement and a closed end is a node. This is the reverse of what students expect from the word 'closed'."),
        MISTAKE("Measuring wavelength between a node and an antinode", r"Adjacent nodes are $\lambda/2$ apart, and a node to the next antinode is only $\lambda/4$. Always say which you measure."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that the particles move", "Nodes do not move at all, but every other point vibrates. A stationary wave does not mean nothing is moving. Between two adjacent nodes all points oscillate in phase."),
        QUIZ(r"A string fixed at both ends vibrates in its second harmonic. If the length is $L$, what is the wavelength?",
             [r"$\dfrac{L}{2}$", r"$L$", r"$2L$", r"$\dfrac{2L}{3}$"], 1,
             r"The second harmonic has two loops, so $L = 2 \times \dfrac{\lambda}{2} = \lambda$.",
             hint="Count the half-wavelength loops: n = 2.",
             feedback=["That is for four loops.", "", "That is the wavelength of the fundamental ($n = 1$).", "That is the wavelength of the third harmonic."]),
        QUIZ(r"A pipe closed at one end has fundamental frequency $200\ \text{Hz}$. Which of these is also a resonant frequency of the same pipe?",
             [r"$400\ \text{Hz}$", r"$500\ \text{Hz}$", r"$600\ \text{Hz}$", r"$800\ \text{Hz}$"], 2,
             r"A closed pipe has only odd harmonics: $200, 600, 1000, \dots\ \text{Hz}$.",
             hint="Which multiples of the fundamental are allowed in a closed pipe?",
             feedback=["That is the second harmonic, which a closed pipe does not have.", "500 is not a whole-number multiple of 200.", "", "That is the fourth harmonic, an even one, which is not allowed."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A guitar string vibrates with fundamental frequency $330\ \text{Hz}$ when its vibrating length is $0.65\ \text{m}$. A player presses the string so that the vibrating length is $0.50\ \text{m}$ (the tension is unchanged). Find the new fundamental frequency.",
           r"$f_1 = \dfrac{v}{2L}$ with $v$ unchanged, so $f_1 \propto \dfrac{1}{L}$. $f_1' = 330 \times \dfrac{0.65}{0.50} = 429\ \text{Hz}$."),
        PQ(r"In a resonance-tube experiment a tuning fork of $384\ \text{Hz}$ gives resonance at air column lengths $0.215\ \text{m}$ and $0.665\ \text{m}$. Find the speed of sound and the end correction.",
           r"$\dfrac{\lambda}{2} = 0.665 - 0.215 = 0.450\ \text{m}$, so $\lambda = 0.900\ \text{m}$ and $v = 384 \times 0.900 = 346\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. $e = \dfrac{\lambda}{4} - L_1 = 0.225 - 0.215 = 0.010\ \text{m}$."),
        PQ("State three differences between a progressive wave and a stationary wave.",
           "(1) In a progressive wave every point has the same amplitude; in a stationary wave the amplitude varies from zero at the nodes to a maximum at the antinodes. (2) A progressive wave transfers energy along the medium; a stationary wave does not transfer energy. (3) In a progressive wave the phase changes continuously along the wave; in a stationary wave all points between adjacent nodes are in phase and points in neighbouring loops are in antiphase."),
        PQ(r"Explain why a pipe closed at one end of length $0.30\ \text{m}$ produces a lower note than a pipe open at both ends of the same length, and why the open pipe has a richer sound.",
           r"For the closed pipe the longest wave that fits has $L = \dfrac{\lambda}{4}$, so $\lambda = 4L = 1.2\ \text{m}$ and $f_1 = \dfrac{v}{4L} = 283\ \text{Hz}$. For the open pipe $L = \dfrac{\lambda}{2}$, $\lambda = 0.60\ \text{m}$, $f_1 = 567\ \text{Hz}$, twice as high. The open pipe supports all harmonics ($f_1, 2f_1, 3f_1, \dots$), while the closed pipe supports only odd harmonics, so the open pipe gives a mixture with more components."),
        TIP("In the exam: stationary waves are tested in Paper 1 (harmonics of pipes and strings), in Paper 2 (derive the harmonics, compare with progressive waves, resonance tube data) and in the practical Paper 3 (resonance tube or sonometer experiments). Always sketch the pattern with N and A before you write the equation."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"A stationary wave is made by two equal waves travelling in opposite directions: $y = 2A\cos kx\sin\omega t$. It has nodes (zero amplitude) and antinodes; adjacent nodes are $\dfrac{\lambda}{2}$ apart.",
            r"String fixed at both ends: $f_n = \dfrac{nv}{2L}$, all harmonics, with $v = \sqrt{F_T/\mu}$.",
            r"Pipe closed at one end: $f = (2n - 1)\dfrac{v}{4L}$ (odd harmonics only). Pipe open at both ends: $f = n\dfrac{v}{2L}$.",
            r"Resonance tube: $L_2 - L_1 = \dfrac{\lambda}{2}$, so $v = 2f(L_2 - L_1)$, and the end correction $e = \dfrac{\lambda}{4} - L_1$.",
            "A stationary wave stores energy; a progressive wave transfers it."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8. Doppler effect
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1,
    topic="The Doppler effect",
    title="The Doppler effect for sound and light",
    slug="phl2-doppler-effect",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Derive the observed frequency when a source or an observer moves, use f' = f(v ± vo)/(v ∓ vs), and apply the effect to sirens, radar, ultrasound and the red shift.",
    objectives=[
        "Explain the Doppler effect with a wavefront diagram",
        "Derive the observed frequency for a moving source and for a moving observer",
        "Use the general formula with the correct signs",
        "Describe applications: speed measurement, medical ultrasound and the red shift of light",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Doppler effect", "Moving source", "Moving observer", "Wavefront compression", "Frequency shift", "Red shift"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Stand beside a main road in Yaoundé as a moto-taxi sounds its horn and passes. The pitch of the horn is higher while it approaches and suddenly drops as it passes. The horn is producing the same note all the time. What changes is the frequency **you receive**. This is the **Doppler effect**: a change in the observed frequency caused by relative motion of the source and the observer."),
        D(r"The **Doppler effect** is the change in the frequency (and wavelength) of a wave detected by an observer when there is relative motion between the source of the wave and the observer along the line joining them.", title="Doppler effect"),
        H("A moving source: wavefronts bunch up"),
        IMG("al-phl2-doppler-wavefronts.svg", alt="Circular wavefronts from a source moving right at half the wave speed: close together ahead of the source, far apart behind it. Observer A, ahead, receives a shorter wavelength and higher frequency; observer B, behind, a longer wavelength and lower frequency.", caption="Figure 1. Each wavefront is a circle centred on the place where the source was when it sent the wave. The moving source follows its own waves in front, so they bunch up."),
        T(r"Let the source emit waves of frequency $f$ (period $T = 1/f$). Let the wave speed relative to the medium (the air) be $v$, and the source speed be $v_s$. In one period the wave crest moves a distance $vT$, and the source moves $v_sT$ in the same time."),
        T(r"**Ahead of the source** the two crests are separated by $\lambda' = vT - v_sT = (v - v_s)T$. The wave speed is still $v$ (it depends only on the medium), so the frequency received by a stationary observer is"),
        F(r"f' = \frac{v}{\lambda'} = \frac{v}{(v - v_s)T} = f\,\frac{v}{v - v_s} \qquad(\text{source approaching})"),
        T(r"**Behind the source** the separation is $(v + v_s)T$, so $f' = f\,\dfrac{v}{v + v_s}$ (source receding)."),
        H("A moving observer"),
        T(r"Now keep the source at rest and move the observer towards it at speed $v_o$. The wavelength $\lambda = v/f$ is unchanged, but the observer meets the crests faster: their speed relative to the observer is $v + v_o$. So $f' = \dfrac{v + v_o}{\lambda}$:"),
        F(r"f' = f\,\frac{v + v_o}{v} \quad(\text{observer approaching}), \qquad f' = f\,\frac{v - v_o}{v} \quad(\text{observer receding})"),
        D(r"When both source and observer move, along the line joining them, with speeds measured relative to the medium,", title="General Doppler formula for sound", formula=r"f' = f\,\frac{v \pm v_o}{v \mp v_s}",
          note="Upper signs: observer moving towards the source and source moving towards the observer. Lower signs: moving apart. The speeds v_o and v_s must be less than v."),
        TIP("Memory trick: moving closer always raises the observed frequency. So choose the sign that makes the answer bigger when they approach (plus on top, minus on the bottom) and smaller when they recede."),
        T(r"**Light and other waves.** Light needs no medium and has speed $c$ for all observers, so the formula above is replaced by a different one. For speeds $v \ll c$ the fractional change in wavelength is simply $\dfrac{\Delta\lambda}{\lambda} \approx \dfrac{v}{c}$, with an increase in wavelength (red shift) for a receding source and a decrease (blue shift) for an approaching source. Astronomers find that the lines in the spectra of distant galaxies are shifted towards the red, which shows that the galaxies are receding."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a moving source", intro=r"A moto-taxi horn emits a steady note of $400\ \text{Hz}$. The moto-taxi moves at $20\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ along a straight road. Take the speed of sound as $340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. What frequency does a stationary pedestrian hear as the moto-taxi (a) approaches and (b) recedes?", steps=[
            S("Approaching", math=r"f' = f\,\frac{v}{v - v_s} = 400 \times \frac{340}{320} = 425\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Receding", math=r"f' = f\,\frac{v}{v + v_s} = 400 \times \frac{340}{360} = 378\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Change as it passes", text="The pitch drops by 425 − 378 = 47 Hz, a clearly audible step."),
        ], check=r"400 \times 340/320 = 425.0,\quad 400 \times 340/360 = 377.8"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a moving observer", intro=r"A stationary source emits $500\ \text{Hz}$. A cyclist rides towards it at $15\ \text{m s}^{-1}$, then away from it at the same speed. Use $v = 340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$.", steps=[
            S("Towards the source", math=r"f' = 500 \times \frac{340 + 15}{340} = 522\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Away from the source", math=r"f' = 500 \times \frac{340 - 15}{340} = 478\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Notice the difference from Example 1", text="The shifts are almost symmetric here, +22 Hz and −22 Hz, because only the speed at which crests are met changes, not the wavelength."),
        ], check=r"500 \times 355/340 = 522.06,\quad 500 \times 325/340 = 477.94"),
        EX("Worked example 3: source and observer both moving", intro=r"An ambulance siren emits $700\ \text{Hz}$. The ambulance moves at $25\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. (a) It approaches a cyclist who is riding towards it at $5.0\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. (b) It approaches a cyclist who is riding away from it, in the same direction as the ambulance, at $5.0\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Use $v = 340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$.", steps=[
            S("(a) Approaching both ways: plus on top, minus below", math=r"f' = 700 \times \frac{340 + 5}{340 - 25} = 700 \times \frac{345}{315} = 767\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("(b) Source approaching, observer receding: minus on top, minus below", math=r"f' = 700 \times \frac{340 - 5}{340 - 25} = 700 \times \frac{335}{315} = 744\ \text{Hz}"),
        ], check=r"700 \times 345/315 = 766.7,\quad 700 \times 335/315 = 744.4"),
        EX("Worked example 4: ultrasound and blood flow", intro=r"A medical ultrasound probe sends $2.0\ \text{MHz}$ waves into blood. Blood cells moving at $0.50\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ along the beam reflect the waves back. The speed of the waves in tissue is $1500\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Find the frequency shift of the reflected wave.", steps=[
            S("The cell is a moving observer, then a moving source. Frequency it receives", math=r"f_1 = f\,\frac{v + u}{v}"),
            S("It re-emits at $f_1$ while moving towards the probe", math=r"f_2 = f_1\,\frac{v}{v - u} = f\,\frac{v + u}{v - u}"),
            S("Substitute", math=r"f_2 = 2.0\times10^6 \times \frac{1500.5}{1499.5} = 2.0013\times10^6\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("The shift", math=r"\Delta f \approx \frac{2fu}{v} = \frac{2 \times 2.0\times10^6 \times 0.50}{1500} = 1.3\ \text{kHz}"),
        ], check=r"2\times10^6 \times 0.0006669 = 1333.8"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Thinking the source changes its own frequency", "The source always emits the same frequency. Only the observed frequency changes."),
        MISTAKE("Using the wrong sign", r"Check your answer: an approaching source or observer must give $f' > f$. If your answer is lower, you swapped a plus and a minus."),
        MISTAKE("Using the same formula for source and observer motion", r"A moving source changes the **wavelength** and puts $v \mp v_s$ in the denominator. A moving observer changes only the **rate** at which crests are met and puts $v \pm v_o$ in the numerator."),
        MISTAKE("Speeds above the wave speed", "The formula needs $v_s < v$. If a source moves faster than the waves it outruns them and makes a shock wave (a sonic boom), a different situation."),
        QUIZ(r"A source of frequency $800\ \text{Hz}$ moves directly towards a stationary observer at $20\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. The speed of sound is $340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. What frequency is heard?",
             [r"$755\ \text{Hz}$", r"$850\ \text{Hz}$", r"$847\ \text{Hz}$", r"$800\ \text{Hz}$"], 1,
             r"$f' = 800 \times \dfrac{340}{340 - 20} = 800 \times 1.0625 = 850\ \text{Hz}$.",
             hint="An approaching source gives a higher frequency: use v − vs in the denominator.",
             feedback=[r"That uses $v + v_s$, the receding formula.", "", r"That is $800(340 + 20)/340 = 847$: you used the observer-moving formula for a moving source.", "A moving source changes the frequency heard, so it cannot be unchanged."]),
        QUIZ("Light from a distant galaxy shows a spectral line at a longer wavelength than the same line from a laboratory source. What does this show?",
             ["The galaxy is moving towards us.", "The galaxy is moving away from us.", "The galaxy is stationary.", "The light was refracted."], 1,
             "A longer wavelength (a red shift) means the source is receding.",
             hint="Which way does the wavelength change when a source moves away?",
             feedback=["An approaching source gives a shorter wavelength (a blue shift).", "", "A stationary source would give no shift.", "Refraction changes the speed and wavelength of light, but the frequency shift of a spectral line comes from relative motion."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A train horn of frequency $600\ \text{Hz}$ is heard by a stationary observer. Find the frequency heard when the train moves at $30\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ (a) towards and (b) away from the observer. Use $v = 340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$.",
           r"(a) $f' = 600 \times \dfrac{340}{340 - 30} = 600 \times 1.0968 = 658\ \text{Hz}$. (b) $f' = 600 \times \dfrac{340}{340 + 30} = 600 \times 0.9189 = 551\ \text{Hz}$."),
        PQ(r"A stationary source emits $1000\ \text{Hz}$. An observer moves towards it at $17\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. Find the frequency heard. (Use $v = 340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$.)",
           r"$f' = f\,\dfrac{v + v_o}{v} = 1000 \times \dfrac{357}{340} = 1050\ \text{Hz}$."),
        PQ(r"A siren emits $800\ \text{Hz}$ and a stationary observer hears $850\ \text{Hz}$ as the siren approaches along a straight line. Find the speed of the source ($v = 340\ \text{m s}^{-1}$).",
           r"$850 = 800 \times \dfrac{340}{340 - v_s}$, so $340 - v_s = \dfrac{800 \times 340}{850} = 320$ and $v_s = 20\ \text{m s}^{-1}$."),
        PQ("Light from a galaxy has a hydrogen line observed at 662.9 nm. The same line measured in the laboratory is at 656.3 nm. Is the galaxy approaching or receding? Estimate its speed using Δλ/λ = v/c, with c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m/s.",
           r"The wavelength has increased (red shift), so the galaxy is **receding**. $\dfrac{\Delta\lambda}{\lambda} = \dfrac{662.9 - 656.3}{656.3} = 0.0101$, so $v = 0.0101 \times 3.00\times10^8 = 3.0\times10^{6}\ \text{m s}^{-1}$."),
        TIP("In the exam: Doppler questions appear as calculations in Paper 1 and Paper 2 (choose the right formula and sign), as explanations with wavefront sketches, and as applications (radar speed guns, ultrasound, red shift). Always state which of the source and the observer is moving."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"The Doppler effect: relative motion along the line of sight changes the observed frequency. The source's emitted frequency does not change.",
            r"Moving source: $f' = f\dfrac{v}{v \mp v_s}$ (minus when approaching). Moving observer: $f' = f\dfrac{v \pm v_o}{v}$ (plus when approaching).",
            r"General: $f' = f\dfrac{v \pm v_o}{v \mp v_s}$. Approach gives $f' > f$; recession gives $f' < f$.",
            r"For light and $v \ll c$: $\dfrac{\Delta\lambda}{\lambda} \approx \dfrac{v}{c}$. A red shift means the source is receding.",
            r"Applications: radar speed guns, ultrasound blood-flow measurement, red shift of galaxies."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 9. Refraction at spherical surfaces
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Refraction at spherical surfaces and the lensmaker's formula",
    title="Refraction at a single spherical surface",
    slug="phl2-refraction-spherical-surface",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Derive n1/u + n2/v = (n2 − n1)/R for paraxial rays at a spherical surface, apply it with a clear sign convention, and use it for apparent depth and curved glass ends.",
    objectives=[
        "State and use the real-is-positive sign convention for u, v and R",
        "Derive n1/u + n2/v = (n2 − n1)/R for paraxial rays",
        "Find image position and magnification at a single spherical surface",
        "Explain apparent depth as the case R → ∞",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Refractive index", "Snell's law", "Paraxial rays", "Radius of curvature", "Sign convention", "Apparent depth", "Lateral magnification"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A fish in a pond at a farm near Dschang always looks closer to the surface than it really is. A glass marble makes an upside-down picture of the window. The front of your eye, the cornea, bends light that reaches it. All these are examples of **refraction at a curved surface**, where light passes from one medium into another through a surface that is part of a sphere. This lesson finds one formula that covers all of them, and the next lesson uses it to build the thin lens."),
        H("Snell's law and small angles"),
        D(r"When light passes from a medium of refractive index $n_1$ into one of index $n_2$, the angle of incidence $i$ and the angle of refraction $r$ (both measured from the normal) obey", title="Snell's law", formula=r"n_1\sin i = n_2\sin r",
          note=r"The refractive index of a medium is $n = \dfrac{c}{v}$, where $c$ is the speed of light in a vacuum and $v$ the speed in the medium. For air, $n \approx 1.00$; water $1.33$; glass about $1.5$."),
        T(r"We now restrict ourselves to **paraxial rays**: rays that make small angles with the axis and meet the surface close to the pole. For small angles in radians, $\sin\theta \approx \theta$ and $\tan\theta \approx \theta$, so Snell's law becomes $n_1 i = n_2 r$. This is the only approximation we need."),
        H("Sign convention (real-is-positive)"),
        T(r"Define the **pole** $P$ as the centre of the surface, and the **centre of curvature** $C$ as the centre of the sphere. The **radius of curvature** is $R = PC$. Light travels from left to right. We use the following convention, which is called *real-is-positive*:"),
        KEY("Rules for u, v and R", "Always say which convention you use. Another common one (the Cartesian convention) changes some signs, so check your own textbook.", cards=[
            [r"Object distance $u$", r"Positive when the object is real (in front of the surface, on the side the light comes from)."],
            [r"Image distance $v$", r"Positive for a **real** image (on the far side, where the light goes). Negative for a **virtual** image (on the same side as the object)."],
            [r"Radius $R$", r"Positive when $C$ is on the far side (the surface is convex towards the incoming light). Negative when $C$ is on the incoming side (the surface is concave towards the light)."],
        ]),
        H("Deriving the formula"),
        IMG("al-phl2-spherical-refraction.svg", alt="A convex glass surface with pole P and centre of curvature C. A ray leaves object O, meets the surface at M and is refracted towards the image I. The normal CM is dashed. Distances u, v and R are marked. Not to scale.", caption="Figure 1. A ray from O to M is refracted towards I. Angles are exaggerated; for paraxial rays they are small."),
        T(r"Let the ray $OM$ meet the surface at height $h$ above the axis. Let $\alpha$ be the angle at $O$, $\beta$ the angle at $C$ and $\gamma$ the angle at $I$. For small angles, $\alpha \approx \dfrac{h}{u}$, $\beta \approx \dfrac{h}{R}$ and $\gamma \approx \dfrac{h}{v}$."),
        T(r"In triangle $OMC$ the angle of incidence is an exterior angle: $i = \alpha + \beta$. In triangle $MCI$, $\beta$ is an exterior angle, so $\beta = r + \gamma$, that is $r = \beta - \gamma$. Put these into $n_1i = n_2r$:"),
        F(r"n_1(\alpha + \beta) = n_2(\beta - \gamma) \;\Rightarrow\; n_1\alpha + n_2\gamma = (n_2 - n_1)\beta"),
        T(r"Replace the angles by $\dfrac{h}{u}$, $\dfrac{h}{v}$, $\dfrac{h}{R}$ and cancel $h$:"),
        D(r"For paraxial rays at a spherical surface separating media of refractive indices $n_1$ (object side) and $n_2$ (image side):", title="Refraction at a spherical surface", formula=r"\frac{n_1}{u} + \frac{n_2}{v} = \frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}",
          note="Use the real-is-positive convention. The result does not depend on h, so all paraxial rays from O meet at the same point I: this is why an image forms."),
        T(r"**Magnification.** A ray from the top of an object of height $h_o$ to the pole $P$ makes an angle $\dfrac{h_o}{u}$ with the axis and is refracted at angle $\dfrac{h_i}{v}$, where $h_i$ is the height of the image. Snell's law for small angles gives $n_1\dfrac{h_o}{u} = n_2\dfrac{h_i}{v}$, so"),
        F(r"m = \frac{h_i}{h_o} = \frac{n_1\,v}{n_2\,u}"),
        T(r"A positive $v$ (real image) means the image is inverted; a negative $v$ (virtual image) means it is upright."),
        H("Special cases"),
        KEY("Two useful cases", "", cards=[
            ["Object at infinity", r"$u \to \infty$, so the image forms at $v = \dfrac{n_2R}{n_2 - n_1}$ (the second focal length of the surface)."],
            [r"Plane surface ($R \to \infty$)", r"The right side is zero, so $\dfrac{n_1}{u} + \dfrac{n_2}{v} = 0$ and $v = -\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}u$. The image is virtual and the same side as the object: this gives **apparent depth**."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: a convex glass surface", intro=r"A glass rod ($n = 1.5$) has its end ground to a convex spherical surface of radius $10\ \text{cm}$. A small object in air is $40\ \text{cm}$ from the surface on the axis. Find the image position and the magnification.", steps=[
            S("Identify the quantities", text="$n_1 = 1.0$, $n_2 = 1.5$, $u = +40\\ \\text{cm}$, $R = +10\\ \\text{cm}$ (the centre of curvature is inside the glass, on the far side)."),
            S("Apply the formula", math=r"\frac{1.0}{40} + \frac{1.5}{v} = \frac{1.5 - 1.0}{10} = 0.050"),
            S("Solve for $v$", math=r"\frac{1.5}{v} = 0.050 - 0.025 = 0.025 \Rightarrow v = +60\ \text{cm}"),
            S("Magnification", math=r"m = \frac{n_1v}{n_2u} = \frac{1.0 \times 60}{1.5 \times 40} = 1.0"),
            S("Describe the image", text="Real (v is positive), inverted, 60 cm inside the glass, the same size as the object."),
        ], check=r"\frac{1}{40} + \frac{1.5}{60} = 0.025 + 0.025 = 0.050"),
        EX("Worked example 2: apparent depth", intro=r"A fish pond at a farm is $1.2\ \text{m}$ deep, with water of refractive index $1.33$. How deep does the bottom appear when viewed from directly above?", steps=[
            S("Treat the water surface as a plane ($R \\to \\infty$). The object is in water: $n_1 = 1.33$, $n_2 = 1.00$, $u = 1.2\\ \\text{m}$", math=r"\frac{1.33}{1.2} + \frac{1.00}{v} = 0"),
            S("Solve for $v$", math=r"v = -\frac{1.00 \times 1.2}{1.33} = -0.90\ \text{m}"),
            S("Interpret", text="The negative sign means a virtual image on the water side, 0.90 m below the surface. So apparent depth $= \\dfrac{\\text{real depth}}{n}$: the pond looks shallower than it is, a useful warning for anyone wading in."),
        ], check=r"1.2/1.33 = 0.9023"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a concave surface", intro=r"A glass surface ($n = 1.5$) is concave towards the light, with radius $15\ \text{cm}$. An object in air is $30\ \text{cm}$ from it. Find the image.", steps=[
            S("The centre of curvature is on the incoming side, so $R = -15\\ \\text{cm}$", math=r"\frac{1.0}{30} + \frac{1.5}{v} = \frac{1.5 - 1.0}{-15} = -0.03333"),
            S("Solve", math=r"\frac{1.5}{v} = -0.03333 - 0.03333 = -0.06667 \Rightarrow v = -22.5\ \text{cm}"),
            S("Magnification", math=r"m = \frac{1.0 \times (-22.5)}{1.5 \times 30} = -0.50"),
            S("Describe the image", text="Virtual (v negative), upright, 22.5 cm in front of the surface on the object side, and half the height of the object."),
        ], check=r"\frac{1}{30} + \frac{1.5}{-22.5} = 0.03333 - 0.06667 = -0.03333"),
        EX("Worked example 4: a glass sphere as a lens", intro=r"Parallel light from the Sun falls on a solid glass sphere of radius $5.0\ \text{cm}$ and $n = 1.5$. Where does it focus?", steps=[
            S("First surface: $u \\to \\infty$, $R = +5.0\\ \\text{cm}$", math=r"v_1 = \frac{n_2R}{n_2 - n_1} = \frac{1.5 \times 5.0}{0.5} = 15\ \text{cm}"),
            S("The back surface is $10\\ \\text{cm}$ from the front, so the first image is $5\\ \\text{cm}$ beyond it: a **virtual object** for surface 2, $u_2 = -5.0\\ \\text{cm}$. Now the light goes from glass to air, and $R_2 = -5.0\\ \\text{cm}$ (centre on the incoming side)", math=r"\frac{1.5}{-5.0} + \frac{1.0}{v_2} = \frac{1.0 - 1.5}{-5.0} = 0.10"),
            S("Solve", math=r"\frac{1}{v_2} = 0.10 + 0.30 = 0.40 \Rightarrow v_2 = 2.5\ \text{cm}"),
            S("Conclusion", text="The light focuses 2.5 cm beyond the back of the sphere, 7.5 cm from its centre. This two-surface method is exactly how the lensmaker's formula is derived next."),
        ], check=r"\frac{n R}{2(n - 1)} = \frac{1.5\times5}{2\times0.5} = 7.5\ \text{cm from the centre}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Swapping n1 and n2", r"$n_1$ is the index of the medium the light **starts** in (where the object is). $n_2$ is where the light goes. In the fish pond the object is in water, so $n_1 = 1.33$."),
        MISTAKE("Wrong sign of R", "R is positive when the centre of curvature is on the far side from the object. For a surface that bulges towards the object, R is positive; for one that dips away from it, R is negative."),
        MISTAKE("Dividing apparent depth the wrong way", r"Apparent depth $= \dfrac{\text{real depth}}{n}$ when you look into a denser medium from above. A pool of real depth $2.0\ \text{m}$ looks $1.5\ \text{m}$ deep, not $2.7\ \text{m}$."),
        MISTAKE("Using the formula for wide-angle rays", "The formula holds only for paraxial rays. A wide beam gives a blurred image (spherical aberration)."),
        QUIZ(r"A swimming pool is $2.0\ \text{m}$ deep and filled with water of $n = 1.33$. How deep does it appear when viewed from directly above?",
             [r"$2.7\ \text{m}$", r"$1.5\ \text{m}$", r"$2.0\ \text{m}$", r"$0.67\ \text{m}$"], 1,
             r"Apparent depth $= \dfrac{2.0}{1.33} = 1.5\ \text{m}$.",
             hint="Apparent depth is real depth divided by n.",
             feedback=["You multiplied by n instead of dividing.", "", "That would be the real depth. Refraction makes the pool look shallower.", r"That is $2.0/3$: divide by $n$, not by 3."]),
        QUIZ(r"Parallel light falls on a convex spherical glass surface of $n = 1.5$ and radius $6.0\ \text{cm}$. Where is the image?",
             [r"$6.0\ \text{cm}$ inside the glass", r"$12\ \text{cm}$ inside the glass", r"$18\ \text{cm}$ inside the glass", r"$4.0\ \text{cm}$ inside the glass"], 2,
             r"$v = \dfrac{n_2R}{n_2 - n_1} = \dfrac{1.5 \times 6.0}{0.5} = 18\ \text{cm}$.",
             hint="u is infinite, so use v = n2 R/(n2 − n1).",
             feedback=["That is simply R.", "That would be 2R, which is not the result here.", "", r"You divided $R$ by $n_2$. The correct rearrangement has $n_2R$ in the numerator and $n_2 - n_1$ in the denominator."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A coin lies at the bottom of a glass of water $8.0\ \text{cm}$ deep ($n = 1.33$). How deep does the coin appear when viewed vertically from above?",
           r"Apparent depth $= \dfrac{\text{real depth}}{n} = \dfrac{8.0}{1.33} = 6.0\ \text{cm}$."),
        PQ(r"The end of a glass rod ($n = 1.5$) is a convex surface of radius $2.0\ \text{cm}$. Parallel light travels along the rod axis from the air side and enters the end. How far inside the glass is the image?",
           r"$v = \dfrac{n_2R}{n_2 - n_1} = \dfrac{1.5 \times 2.0}{0.5} = 6.0\ \text{cm}$."),
        PQ(r"An object in air is $20\ \text{cm}$ from a convex water surface of radius $10\ \text{cm}$ ($n = 1.33$). Describe the image formed.",
           r"$\dfrac{1}{20} + \dfrac{1.33}{v} = \dfrac{0.33}{10} = 0.033$, so $\dfrac{1.33}{v} = 0.033 - 0.050 = -0.017$ and $v = -78\ \text{cm}$. The image is virtual (negative $v$), 78 cm from the surface on the object side. Its magnification is $m = \dfrac{1 \times (-78)}{1.33 \times 20} = -2.9$, so it is upright and enlarged about three times."),
        PQ(r"Show that for a plane surface between media of indices $n_1$ (object side) and $n_2$ the image distance is $v = -\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}u$. A window pane is $4.0\ \text{cm}$ thick ($n = 1.5$). How far does a small mark on the far face appear to move when viewed normally through the pane?",
           r"For a plane surface $R \to \infty$, so $\dfrac{n_1}{u} + \dfrac{n_2}{v} = 0$ and $v = -\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}u$. The mark is in glass ($n_1 = 1.5$), viewed from air ($n_2 = 1$): $v = -\dfrac{4.0}{1.5} = -2.67\ \text{cm}$. The mark appears $2.67\ \text{cm}$ behind the near face, that is $4.0 - 2.67 = 1.33\ \text{cm}$ closer to the observer."),
        TIP("In the exam: apparent depth and the single-surface formula appear in Paper 1 (numerical choices) and in Paper 2 (derivations and image position). State your sign convention in the working. This topic is also the foundation of the lens formula in the next lesson."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Paraxial rays: $n_1 i = n_2 r$. The refractive index is $n = c/v$.",
            r"$\dfrac{n_1}{u} + \dfrac{n_2}{v} = \dfrac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$ (real-is-positive: $R > 0$ when $C$ is on the far side; $v < 0$ means a virtual image).",
            r"Magnification $m = \dfrac{n_1v}{n_2u}$; positive $v$ gives an inverted image.",
            r"Plane surface: $v = -\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}u$; apparent depth $= \dfrac{\text{real depth}}{n}$.",
            "Two-surface problems: the first image is the object for the second surface (a virtual object has negative u)."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 10. Lensmaker's formula, thin lenses, mirrors
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Refraction at spherical surfaces and the lensmaker's formula",
    title="The lensmaker's formula, thin lenses and mirrors",
    slug="phl2-lensmaker-thin-lens-mirror",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Derive the lensmaker's formula 1/f = (n − 1)(1/r1 + 1/r2) by two refractions, use 1/u + 1/v = 1/f for lenses and spherical mirrors, and draw standard ray diagrams.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive the thin-lens formula and the lensmaker's formula from two surface refractions",
        "Use 1/u + 1/v = 1/f, magnification and power for lenses and spherical mirrors",
        "Draw the three standard rays and describe the image for each object position",
        "Combine thin lenses in contact by adding powers",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Focal length", "Lensmaker's formula", "Thin-lens formula", "Magnification", "Power in dioptres", "Spherical mirror", "Real and virtual images"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Spectacles for a short-sighted student, the lens of a camera phone, a pharmacist's magnifying glass, and the mirror at the back of a torch are all shaped pieces of glass or metal. This lesson shows how the **focal length** of a lens follows from its shape and its glass, and gives you the formula used for lenses and curved mirrors."),
        H("The thin lens: two refractions"),
        T(r"A **thin lens** has a thickness so small that we measure all distances from its centre. Take a lens of refractive index $n$ in air. Its two surfaces have radii $r_1$ (the surface the light meets first) and $r_2$. We take $r$ **positive for a surface that bulges outwards (convex)** and **negative for one that curves inwards (concave)**. For a biconvex lens both are positive."),
        T(r"In the real-is-positive convention of the last lesson, the second surface of a biconvex lens has its centre of curvature on the incoming side, so its single-surface radius is $-r_2$. Apply the surface formula twice."),
        T(r"**Surface 1** (air to glass): $\dfrac{1}{u} + \dfrac{n}{v_1} = \dfrac{n - 1}{r_1}$, where $v_1$ is the position of the first image."),
        T(r"**Surface 2** (glass to air): the first image is the object, at distance $-v_1$ (this is a virtual object if $v_1 > 0$, and the lens is thin). So $\dfrac{n}{-v_1} + \dfrac{1}{v} = \dfrac{1 - n}{-r_2} = \dfrac{n - 1}{r_2}$."),
        T(r"Add the two equations. The terms $\dfrac{n}{v_1}$ and $-\dfrac{n}{v_1}$ cancel:"),
        F(r"\frac{1}{u} + \frac{1}{v} = (n - 1)\left(\frac{1}{r_1} + \frac{1}{r_2}\right)"),
        T(r"When the object is at infinity, $\dfrac{1}{u} = 0$ and the image is at the **focal length** $f$ of the lens. So the right-hand side equals $\dfrac{1}{f}$:"),
        D(r"For a thin lens of refractive index $n$ in air, with surface radii $r_1$ and $r_2$ (convex positive, concave negative),", title="Lensmaker's formula", formula=r"\frac{1}{f} = (n - 1)\left(\frac{1}{r_1} + \frac{1}{r_2}\right)",
          note=r"A flat surface has $r = \infty$, so its term is zero. If the lens is in a medium of index $n_m$, replace $n - 1$ by $\dfrac{n}{n_m} - 1$."),
        D(r"For any thin lens or spherical mirror, with real-is-positive distances,", title="Thin-lens and mirror formula", formula=r"\frac{1}{u} + \frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{f}, \qquad m = \frac{h_i}{h_o} = \frac{v}{u}",
          note="f > 0 for a converging lens or a concave mirror; f < 0 for a diverging lens or a convex mirror. For a spherical mirror, f = R/2. A real image (v > 0) is inverted; a virtual image (v < 0) is upright."),
        D(r"The **power** of a lens is $P = \dfrac{1}{f}$, with $f$ in metres. Its unit is the **dioptre** (D), $1\ \text{D} = 1\ \text{m}^{-1}$. For thin lenses in contact the powers add:", title="Power and lenses in contact", formula=r"P = P_1 + P_2 \quad\Leftrightarrow\quad \frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{f_1} + \frac{1}{f_2}",
          note="The first lens makes an image that is the object for the second, exactly like the two surfaces above."),
        H("Ray diagrams"),
        KEY("The three standard rays", "Draw two or three of these from the top of the object. Where they meet (or appear to meet) is the top of the image.", cards=[
            ["Parallel ray", r"A ray parallel to the axis passes through (or appears to come from) the **focal point** after the lens or mirror."],
            ["Ray through the focus", r"A ray through the focal point on the object side emerges **parallel** to the axis."],
            ["Ray through the centre", r"For a lens, a ray through the centre passes **straight on**. For a concave mirror, a ray to the pole reflects at an equal angle."],
        ]),
        IMG("al-phl2-lens-rays.svg", alt="Converging lens with focal points F and 2F marked on each side. An object arrow lies between F and 2F on the left. Three rays from its tip meet beyond 2F on the right, forming a larger inverted real image.", caption="Figure 1. Converging lens, object between F and 2F: image real, inverted, magnified, beyond 2F."),
        IMG("al-phl2-mirror-rays.svg", alt="A concave mirror with pole P, focal point F and centre of curvature C on the axis. An object arrow between F and C. Three reflected rays meet beyond C to form a larger inverted real image.", caption="Figure 2. Concave mirror, object between F and C: image real, inverted, magnified, beyond C."),
        CODE("""Converging lens (or concave mirror), focal length f
Object position       Image position       Image
beyond 2f             between f and 2f     real, inverted, smaller
at 2f                 at 2f                real, inverted, same size
between f and 2f      beyond 2f            real, inverted, larger
at f                  at infinity          (parallel rays emerge)
inside f              same side as object  virtual, upright, larger
(Diverging lens or convex mirror: always virtual, upright, smaller.)""", "text", title="Images formed by a converging lens"),
        EX("Worked example 1: lensmaker's formula", intro=r"A biconvex lens has both faces of radius $20\ \text{cm}$ and is made of glass of $n = 1.5$. Find its focal length and power. What is the focal length if one face is made flat?", steps=[
            S("Substitute", math=r"\frac{1}{f} = (1.5 - 1)\left(\frac{1}{20} + \frac{1}{20}\right) = 0.5 \times 0.10 = 0.050\ \text{cm}^{-1}"),
            S("Focal length and power", math=r"f = 20\ \text{cm} = 0.20\ \text{m},\quad P = \frac{1}{0.20} = +5.0\ \text{D}"),
            S("Plano-convex: one radius infinite", math=r"\frac{1}{f} = 0.5\left(\frac{1}{20} + 0\right) = 0.025 \Rightarrow f = 40\ \text{cm}"),
        ], check=r"0.5 \times 0.10 = 0.05;\quad 1/0.025 = 40"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a real image", intro=r"A converging lens has $f = 20\ \text{cm}$. An object is placed $30\ \text{cm}$ from the lens. Find the image position and magnification.", steps=[
            S("Rearrange the lens formula", math=r"\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{f} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{20} - \frac{1}{30} = \frac{1}{60}"),
            S("Image distance", math=r"v = +60\ \text{cm}"),
            S("Magnification", math=r"m = \frac{v}{u} = \frac{60}{30} = 2.0"),
            S("Describe", text="Real (v positive), inverted, twice the size of the object, 60 cm from the lens on the far side. This is the situation in Figure 1."),
        ], check=r"\frac{1}{30} + \frac{1}{60} = \frac{3}{60} = \frac{1}{20}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a diverging lens", intro=r"A diverging lens has $f = -20\ \text{cm}$. An object is $30\ \text{cm}$ from it. Describe the image.", steps=[
            S("Lens formula", math=r"\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{f} - \frac{1}{u} = -\frac{1}{20} - \frac{1}{30} = -\frac{5}{60}"),
            S("Image distance and magnification", math=r"v = -12\ \text{cm},\qquad m = \frac{v}{u} = -0.40"),
            S("Describe", text="Virtual (v negative), upright, 12 cm from the lens on the object side, and 0.40 times the object size. A diverging lens always gives this kind of image."),
        ], check=r"-\frac{1}{20} - \frac{1}{30} = -\frac{1}{12}"),
        EX("Worked example 4: mirrors", intro=r"A concave mirror has radius of curvature $40\ \text{cm}$. Find the image for an object at (a) $50\ \text{cm}$ and (b) $10\ \text{cm}$. Then find the image in a convex lorry mirror of $f = -50\ \text{cm}$ for an object $100\ \text{cm}$ away.", steps=[
            S("Focal length of the concave mirror", math=r"f = \frac{R}{2} = 20\ \text{cm}"),
            S("(a) $u = 50\\ \\text{cm}$", math=r"\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{20} - \frac{1}{50} = \frac{3}{100} \Rightarrow v = 33\ \text{cm},\ m = 0.67\ \text{(real, inverted, smaller)}"),
            S("(b) $u = 10\\ \\text{cm}$ (inside $f$): like a shaving mirror", math=r"\frac{1}{v} = \frac{1}{20} - \frac{1}{10} = -\frac{1}{20} \Rightarrow v = -20\ \text{cm},\ m = 2.0\ \text{(virtual, upright, larger)}"),
            S("(c) Convex mirror, $f = -50\\ \\text{cm}$, $u = 100\\ \\text{cm}$", math=r"\frac{1}{v} = -\frac{1}{50} - \frac{1}{100} = -\frac{3}{100} \Rightarrow v = -33\ \text{cm},\ m = 0.33"),
            S("Comment", text="A convex mirror always gives a virtual, upright, smaller image with a wide field of view. That is why it is used as a wing mirror."),
        ], check=r"\frac{1}{50} + \frac{1}{33.33} = 0.05"),
        T(r"**A lens in water.** The formula with $\dfrac{n}{n_m} - 1$ shows that the focal length depends on the surrounding medium. The $20\ \text{cm}$ glass lens of Example 1 has $\dfrac{1}{f} = \left(\dfrac{1.5}{1.33} - 1\right) \times 0.10$ in water, giving $f = 78\ \text{cm}$: nearly four times longer than in air. A fish's eye lens is almost spherical to make up for this."),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Wrong sign for f or v", "f is positive for a converging lens and a concave mirror, negative for a diverging lens and a convex mirror. A negative v means a virtual image: do not discard the sign."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing units", r"Use the same length unit for $u$, $v$ and $f$. For power in dioptres, $f$ must be in **metres**: $f = 20\ \text{cm}$ is $P = +5\ \text{D}$, not $+0.05\ \text{D}$."),
        MISTAKE("Drawing rays that miss the focal point", "On a ray diagram the parallel ray must go through the focal point F on the far side (lens) and the ray through the near focus must come out parallel. Use a ruler and mark F and 2F on both sides."),
        MISTAKE("Using the lensmaker formula for a concave face", r"A concave face has a **negative** radius. For a meniscus or biconcave lens, put the sign in: a biconcave glass lens with $r_1 = r_2 = 20\ \text{cm}$ has $\dfrac{1}{f} = 0.5(-0.05 - 0.05) = -0.05$, so $f = -20\ \text{cm}$."),
        QUIZ(r"An object is placed $12\ \text{cm}$ from a converging lens of focal length $20\ \text{cm}$. Which describes the image?",
             ["Real, inverted, magnified", "Virtual, upright, magnified", "Virtual, upright, diminished", "Real, upright, magnified"], 1,
             r"The object is inside $f$. $\dfrac{1}{v} = \dfrac{1}{20} - \dfrac{1}{12} = -\dfrac{1}{30}$, so $v = -30\ \text{cm}$: a virtual, upright image, $m = 2.5$ (a magnifying glass).",
             hint="Is the object nearer than the focal length?",
             feedback=["A real image needs the object beyond the focal point.", "", "A virtual image from a converging lens is always magnified. Only a diverging lens gives a diminished virtual image.", "Real images are always inverted."]),
        QUIZ(r"Two thin lenses of powers $+4.0\ \text{D}$ and $-1.5\ \text{D}$ are placed in contact. What is the focal length of the combination?",
             [r"$+0.25\ \text{m}$", r"$+0.40\ \text{m}$", r"$+2.5\ \text{m}$", r"$-0.40\ \text{m}$"], 1,
             r"$P = 4.0 + (-1.5) = +2.5\ \text{D}$, so $f = \dfrac{1}{2.5} = +0.40\ \text{m}$.",
             hint="Add the powers, then take the reciprocal.",
             feedback=["That is the focal length of the +4.0 D lens alone.", "", "That is the power in dioptres, not a focal length in metres.", "The net power is positive, so the combination is converging."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A biconvex lens of glass of $n = 1.6$ has two faces of equal radius and focal length $15\ \text{cm}$. Find the radius of each face.",
           r"$\dfrac{1}{f} = (n - 1)\dfrac{2}{r}$, so $r = 2(n - 1)f = 2 \times 0.6 \times 15 = 18\ \text{cm}$."),
        PQ(r"A slide projector uses a converging lens of focal length $10\ \text{cm}$. The slide is $10.5\ \text{cm}$ from the lens. Where is the screen, and how large is the image of a $24\ \text{mm}$ slide?",
           r"$\dfrac{1}{v} = \dfrac{1}{10} - \dfrac{1}{10.5} = \dfrac{0.5}{105}$, so $v = 210\ \text{cm}$: the screen is $2.1\ \text{m}$ from the lens. $m = \dfrac{210}{10.5} = 20$, so the image is $20 \times 24 = 480\ \text{mm}$ high (inverted)."),
        PQ(r"An object is $15\ \text{cm}$ in front of a concave mirror of focal length $10\ \text{cm}$. Find the image distance and magnification, and say whether the image is real or virtual.",
           r"$\dfrac{1}{v} = \dfrac{1}{10} - \dfrac{1}{15} = \dfrac{1}{30}$, so $v = 30\ \text{cm}$ and $m = \dfrac{30}{15} = 2.0$. The image is real (v positive), inverted and twice the size of the object."),
        PQ("Draw a ray diagram for a diverging lens with an object beyond the focal length, and describe the image.",
           r"Mark F on both sides. Draw (1) a ray parallel to the axis that bends so that it appears to come from the focal point on the object side; (2) a ray towards the far focal point, which emerges parallel to the axis; (3) a ray through the centre that goes straight on. The diverging rays never meet; extend them backwards to meet at a point on the object side. The image is virtual, upright, diminished, and lies between the lens and the focal point."),
        TIP("In the exam: lens and mirror calculations appear in Paper 1 and Paper 2, and ray diagrams are drawn in Paper 2 structured questions. Mark F and 2F with a ruler, put arrows on the rays, and state whether the image is real or virtual. The focal length of a converging lens is also a practical Paper 3 measurement."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Lensmaker's formula: $\dfrac{1}{f} = (n - 1)\left(\dfrac{1}{r_1} + \dfrac{1}{r_2}\right)$ (convex surfaces positive); in a medium use $\dfrac{n}{n_m} - 1$.",
            r"Thin lens and spherical mirror: $\dfrac{1}{u} + \dfrac{1}{v} = \dfrac{1}{f}$, $m = \dfrac{v}{u}$, and $f = \dfrac{R}{2}$ for a mirror.",
            r"$f > 0$ for converging lens or concave mirror; $f < 0$ for diverging lens or convex mirror; $v < 0$ means a virtual, upright image.",
            r"Power $P = \dfrac{1}{f}$ (dioptres, $f$ in metres); lenses in contact: $P = P_1 + P_2$.",
            "Standard rays: parallel to the axis through F; through F emerging parallel; through the centre straight on."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 11. Optical instruments
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Optical instruments",
    title="The eye, magnifier, microscope and telescope",
    slug="phl2-optical-instruments",
    minutes=26,
    summary="Define angular magnification, correct short and long sight, and find the magnifying power and lens separation of the simple magnifier, compound microscope and astronomical telescope.",
    objectives=[
        "Define angular magnification and the near point",
        "Correct short sight and long sight with the lens formula",
        "Calculate the magnification of a magnifier, a compound microscope and a telescope",
        "State how the aperture limits the detail an instrument can resolve",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Near point", "Angular magnification", "Short sight and long sight", "Simple magnifier", "Compound microscope", "Astronomical telescope", "Normal adjustment", "Resolution"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A laboratory technician in Bamenda uses a microscope to count parasites in a blood slide. A student at a school observatory points a telescope at Jupiter. A grandmother uses reading glasses. All of them use lenses to make the image on the retina of the eye **larger**. This lesson builds on the thin-lens formula to explain how."),
        H("The eye and its defects"),
        T(r"The eye's lens forms a real image on the retina. By changing its shape (accommodation) it can focus objects from the **far point** (infinity for a normal eye) to the **near point**, the closest distance for clear vision. For a normal adult the near point is taken as $D = 25\ \text{cm}$ (a standard value; individual eyes differ)."),
        KEY("Two common defects", "A correcting lens is chosen so that it makes a virtual image at the eye's own far or near point.", cards=[
            ["Short sight (myopia)", r"The far point is closer than infinity. A **diverging** lens makes a distant object appear (virtual image) at the far point: $f = -(\text{far point distance})$."],
            ["Long sight (hypermetropia)", r"The near point is farther than $25\ \text{cm}$. A **converging** lens makes an object at $25\ \text{cm}$ appear (virtual image) at the near point: $\dfrac{1}{f} = \dfrac{1}{0.25} - \dfrac{1}{N}$."],
        ]),
        D(r"The **angular magnification** (magnifying power) of an instrument is $M = \dfrac{\beta}{\alpha}$, where $\beta$ is the angle subtended at the eye by the final image and $\alpha$ is the angle subtended at the unaided eye by the object when it is at the near point (for a telescope, the object itself).", title="Angular magnification"),
        H("The simple magnifier"),
        T(r"A converging lens of short focal length $f$ held close to the eye, with the object just inside $f$. The unaided object at $D$ subtends $\alpha = \dfrac{h}{D}$. With the image at infinity (relaxed eye, object at the focal point), $\beta = \dfrac{h}{f}$, so $M = \dfrac{D}{f}$. With the image at the near point, $v = -D$ and $\dfrac{1}{u} = \dfrac{1}{f} + \dfrac{1}{D}$, giving $M = \dfrac{D}{u} = 1 + \dfrac{D}{f}$."),
        F(r"M_\infty = \frac{D}{f}, \qquad M_{near} = 1 + \frac{D}{f}"),
        H("The compound microscope"),
        T(r"Two converging lenses: an **objective** of very short focal length $f_o$ and an **eyepiece** of focal length $f_e$. The object is just beyond $f_o$, so the objective forms a real, inverted, enlarged intermediate image of linear magnification $m_o = \dfrac{v_o}{u_o}$. The eyepiece acts as a magnifier on this image. For the final image at infinity:"),
        F(r"M = m_o \times \frac{D}{f_e} = \frac{v_o}{u_o}\times\frac{D}{f_e}, \qquad \text{lens separation} = v_o + f_e"),
        H("The astronomical telescope"),
        IMG("al-phl2-telescope.svg", alt="Two converging lenses. Parallel rays from a distant object at angle a pass through the objective, which forms an intermediate image at the common focal point. The eyepiece turns the rays into a parallel beam leaving at a larger angle b.", caption="Figure 1. A telescope in normal adjustment: the focal points of the two lenses coincide."),
        T(r"In **normal adjustment** the final image is at infinity, so the focal points of the lenses coincide and the separation is $f_o + f_e$. The object subtends $\alpha \approx \dfrac{h'}{f_o}$ (where $h'$ is the intermediate image height) and the final image subtends $\beta \approx \dfrac{h'}{f_e}$. So"),
        F(r"M = \frac{\beta}{\alpha} = \frac{f_o}{f_e}"),
        T(r"The final image is inverted, which does not matter for stars. A large **aperture** (diameter of the objective, or of a mirror in a reflecting telescope) collects more light and shows finer detail."),
        H("Resolution"),
        T(r"Diffraction limits how close two objects can be and still be seen separately. The **Rayleigh criterion** says that two point sources are just resolved when the central maximum of one diffraction pattern falls on the first minimum of the other. For a circular aperture of diameter $a$ and light of wavelength $\lambda$ the smallest angular separation is"),
        F(r"\theta_{min} = 1.22\,\frac{\lambda}{a}"),
        EX("Worked example 1: correcting eyes", intro=r"(a) A short-sighted student cannot see clearly beyond $2.0\ \text{m}$. Find the lens needed. (b) A long-sighted person has a near point at $75\ \text{cm}$. Find the reading lens so that a book at $25\ \text{cm}$ is seen clearly.", steps=[
            S("(a) Object at infinity must give a virtual image at the far point, $v = -2.0\\ \\text{m}$", math=r"\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{\infty} + \frac{1}{-2.0} \Rightarrow f = -2.0\ \text{m},\quad P = -0.50\ \text{D}"),
            S("(b) $u = 0.25\\ \\text{m}$, $v = -0.75\\ \\text{m}$", math=r"\frac{1}{f} = \frac{1}{0.25} - \frac{1}{0.75} = 2.67\ \text{D} \Rightarrow f = 0.375\ \text{m}"),
            S("State the lens types", text="(a) a diverging lens of power −0.50 D; (b) a converging lens of power +2.7 D."),
        ], check=r"4.00 - 1.33 = 2.67"),
        EX("Worked example 2: simple magnifier", intro=r"A magnifier has $f = 5.0\ \text{cm}$. Find the magnifying power with the image at infinity and at the near point ($D = 25\ \text{cm}$).", steps=[
            S("Image at infinity", math=r"M = \frac{D}{f} = \frac{25}{5.0} = 5.0"),
            S("Image at the near point", math=r"M = 1 + \frac{D}{f} = 6.0"),
        ], check=r"1 + 5 = 6"),
        EX("Worked example 3: a compound microscope", intro=r"A microscope has an objective of $f_o = 0.80\ \text{cm}$ and an eyepiece of $f_e = 4.0\ \text{cm}$. An object is $0.90\ \text{cm}$ from the objective. The final image is at infinity. Find $M$ and the distance between the lenses.", steps=[
            S("Objective image distance", math=r"\frac{1}{v_o} = \frac{1}{0.80} - \frac{1}{0.90} \Rightarrow v_o = 7.2\ \text{cm}"),
            S("Objective magnification", math=r"m_o = \frac{7.2}{0.90} = 8.0"),
            S("Eyepiece magnification (image at infinity)", math=r"\frac{D}{f_e} = \frac{25}{4.0} = 6.25"),
            S("Total", math=r"M = 8.0 \times 6.25 = 50"),
            S("Separation", math=r"v_o + f_e = 7.2 + 4.0 = 11.2\ \text{cm}"),
        ], check=r"8 \times 6.25 = 50"),
        EX("Worked example 4: a telescope and the Moon", intro=r"A telescope has $f_o = 80\ \text{cm}$ and $f_e = 4.0\ \text{cm}$. The Moon subtends $0.0090\ \text{rad}$. Find $M$, the lens separation and the angle subtended by the final image.", steps=[
            S("Magnification", math=r"M = \frac{f_o}{f_e} = \frac{80}{4.0} = 20"),
            S("Separation", math=r"f_o + f_e = 84\ \text{cm}"),
            S("Final angle", math=r"\beta = M\alpha = 20 \times 0.0090 = 0.18\ \text{rad} \approx 10^\circ"),
        ], check=r"0.18 \times 180/\pi = 10.3"),
        EX("Worked example 5: resolution", intro=r"A telescope objective has diameter $0.10\ \text{m}$. Find the smallest angle it can resolve for light of wavelength $550\ \text{nm}$.", steps=[
            S("Rayleigh criterion", math=r"\theta_{min} = 1.22\times\frac{550\times10^{-9}}{0.10} = 6.7\times10^{-6}\ \text{rad}"),
            S("Compare with the eye", text="A pupil of 3.0 mm gives about 0.00022 rad, 33 times worse. A larger aperture resolves finer detail."),
        ], check=r"1.22 \times 5.5\times10^{-6} = 6.71\times10^{-6}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using M = D/f for the telescope", r"The telescope uses $M = f_o/f_e$. $D/f$ is only for magnifiers and microscope eyepieces."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing the lens signs for eye defects", "Short sight needs a diverging lens (negative power). Long sight needs a converging lens (positive power)."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the separation formula", r"Telescope in normal adjustment: $f_o + f_e$. Microscope with final image at infinity: $v_o + f_e$, not $f_o + f_e$."),
        QUIZ(r"A telescope in normal adjustment has an objective of focal length $1.20\ \text{m}$ and an eyepiece of $4.0\ \text{cm}$. What is its angular magnification?",
             [r"$30$", r"$0.033$", r"$48$", r"$1.24$"], 0,
             r"$M = \dfrac{f_o}{f_e} = \dfrac{120}{4.0} = 30$.", hint="Use the same units for both focal lengths.",
             feedback=["", r"That is $f_e/f_o$, upside down.", r"That is $f_o \times f_e$.", "That is the lens separation in metres, not the magnification."]),
        QUIZ(r"A person needs a lens of power $-1.25\ \text{D}$ to see distant objects clearly. What is the far point of the unaided eye?",
             [r"$1.25\ \text{m}$", r"$0.80\ \text{m}$", r"$0.25\ \text{m}$", "At infinity"], 1,
             r"$f = \dfrac{1}{-1.25} = -0.80\ \text{m}$, and the far point equals $|f| = 0.80\ \text{m}$.", hint="Far point = |f| for a short-sighted eye.",
             feedback=["That is the power's size, not the distance.", "", "That is the near point of a normal eye in metres.", "A normal eye has an infinite far point; this eye needs correction, so it does not."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A magnifying glass has $f = 8.0\ \text{cm}$. Find $M$ with the image at infinity and with the image at the near point of $25\ \text{cm}$.",
           r"$M_\infty = \dfrac{25}{8.0} = 3.1$. $M_{near} = 1 + \dfrac{25}{8.0} = 4.1$."),
        PQ(r"A microscope objective forms an intermediate image $10\ \text{cm}$ from it of an object $1.0\ \text{cm}$ from it. The eyepiece has $f_e = 5.0\ \text{cm}$ and the final image is at infinity. Find $M$.",
           r"$m_o = \dfrac{10}{1.0} = 10$. Eyepiece: $\dfrac{25}{5.0} = 5.0$. $M = 10 \times 5.0 = 50$."),
        PQ(r"An astronomical telescope in normal adjustment has an angular magnification of $30$ and a length of $1.24\ \text{m}$. Find the focal lengths of the two lenses.",
           r"$\dfrac{f_o}{f_e} = 30$ and $f_o + f_e = 1.24$, so $31f_e = 1.24$ and $f_e = 0.040\ \text{m}$, $f_o = 1.20\ \text{m}$."),
        PQ(r"Explain why a larger-diameter objective lens helps astronomers separate two close stars.",
           r"Diffraction at the circular aperture spreads each star's image into a pattern. By the Rayleigh criterion the smallest resolvable angle is $1.22\lambda/a$, which decreases as the aperture diameter $a$ increases. A larger aperture therefore resolves closer stars (and also collects more light)."),
        TIP("In the exam: instrument questions in Paper 1 and Paper 2 combine the lens formula with M formulas. State whether the final image is at infinity (normal adjustment) or at the near point. Always give the lens separation if asked."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Near point $D = 25\ \text{cm}$. Short sight: diverging lens with $f = -$(far point). Long sight: converging lens making the object at 25 cm appear at the near point.",
            r"Magnifier: $M = D/f$ (image at infinity), $1 + D/f$ (image at near point).",
            r"Microscope: $M = \dfrac{v_o}{u_o}\times\dfrac{D}{f_e}$; separation $v_o + f_e$.",
            r"Telescope (normal adjustment): $M = f_o/f_e$; separation $f_o + f_e$.",
            r"Resolution: $\theta_{min} = 1.22\lambda/a$."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 12. Huygens' principle
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Huygens' principle and Young's double-slit interference",
    title="Huygens' principle, superposition and diffraction",
    slug="phl2-huygens-principle",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Use Huygens' wavelets to explain reflection, refraction (sin i / sin r = v1/v2) and diffraction, state the conditions for interference, and find the single-slit minimum a sin θ = λ.",
    objectives=[
        "State Huygens' principle and use it to construct a new wavefront",
        "Derive the law of refraction and n = c/v from the wavelet construction",
        "State the conditions for constructive and destructive interference",
        "Use a sin θ = λ for the first minimum of single-slit diffraction",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Wavefront", "Huygens' principle", "Secondary wavelets", "Coherence", "Path difference", "Superposition", "Single-slit diffraction"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Why does a ripple spread out into a wide arc after passing through a small gap in a wall? Why does the sound of a radio reach you around the corner of a building while the light from the lamp does not? In the 17th century Christiaan Huygens gave a simple rule that answers both questions and underlies wave optics."),
        H("Huygens' principle"),
        D(r"A **wavefront** is a line or surface joining all points that are in the same state of vibration (same phase). **Huygens' principle**: every point on a wavefront can be regarded as a source of secondary **wavelets** spreading out in the forward direction with the speed of the wave. The new wavefront after time $t$ is the **envelope** (the common tangent) of these wavelets.", title="Huygens' principle"),
        T(r"For a plane wave, the wavelets from points along a straight wavefront have radius $vt$ after time $t$, and the tangent to them is a new straight wavefront a distance $vt$ ahead. For a circular wave the envelope is a larger circle."),
        H("Refraction from Huygens' principle"),
        IMG("al-phl2-huygens-refraction.svg", alt="A plane wavefront AB meets a boundary between a faster medium above and a slower medium below. In the time the wave goes from B to C in medium 1 (distance v1 t), the wavelet from A in medium 2 has radius v2 t. The new wavefront is the tangent CT from C to that wavelet. Angles i and r are marked.", caption="Figure 1. Huygens' construction for refraction."),
        T(r"In time $t$ the wavefront at $B$ reaches $C$ in medium 1: $BC = v_1t$. Meanwhile the wavelet from $A$ travels $AT = v_2t$ in medium 2. The angle $i$ at $A$ in triangle $ABC$ satisfies $\sin i = \dfrac{BC}{AC} = \dfrac{v_1t}{AC}$. The angle $r$ at $C$ in triangle $ATC$ satisfies $\sin r = \dfrac{AT}{AC} = \dfrac{v_2t}{AC}$. Dividing,"),
        F(r"\frac{\sin i}{\sin r} = \frac{v_1}{v_2}"),
        T(r"The frequency does not change when a wave crosses a boundary, so $v = f\lambda$ gives $\dfrac{v_1}{v_2} = \dfrac{\lambda_1}{\lambda_2}$. With the refractive index $n = \dfrac{c}{v}$ this is Snell's law, $n_1\sin i = n_2\sin r$, and a wave entering a slower medium bends towards the normal. **Reflection** follows from the same construction in the same medium: $v_1 = v_2$ gives angle of incidence = angle of reflection."),
        H("Superposition and interference"),
        D(r"The **principle of superposition**: when two or more waves meet at a point, the resultant displacement is the vector (algebraic) sum of the displacements the waves would produce separately.", title="Superposition"),
        T(r"Two sources are **coherent** if they have a constant phase difference and the same frequency. Interference patterns that stay still need coherent sources. Two separate lamps are not coherent; light from one lamp divided into two beams (as with two slits) is. For two coherent sources whose waves meet at a point with a **path difference** $\Delta$:"),
        KEY("Conditions for interference", r"Here $m = 0, 1, 2, \dots$ and $\lambda$ is the wavelength. We assume the sources are in phase.", cards=[
            ["Constructive (maximum)", r"$\Delta = m\lambda$. The waves arrive in phase (phase difference $2\pi m$) and the amplitudes add."],
            ["Destructive (minimum)", r"$\Delta = \left(m + \tfrac12\right)\lambda$. The waves arrive in antiphase and cancel if their amplitudes are equal."],
        ]),
        H("Diffraction at a single slit"),
        T(r"**Diffraction** is the spreading of a wave when it passes through a gap or around an obstacle. It is most noticeable when the gap width $a$ is comparable with the wavelength $\lambda$: sound ($\lambda \approx 1\ \text{m}$) bends round doors, but light ($\lambda \approx 5\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}$) needs a slit about a tenth of a millimetre wide to show it."),
        T(r"Huygens' principle explains the pattern. Treat the slit as a row of wavelet sources. For the **first minimum** consider the sources in the slit as two halves. A wavelet from the top edge and one from the middle differ in path by $\dfrac{a}{2}\sin\theta$. If this equals $\dfrac{\lambda}{2}$ they cancel, and every wavelet in the upper half is cancelled by one in the lower half. So the first minimum is at"),
        F(r"a\sin\theta = \lambda"),
        T(r"The **central maximum** is the widest and brightest part of the pattern. Its angular half-width is $\theta \approx \dfrac{\lambda}{a}$ (small angle): a narrower slit gives a **wider** pattern, and longer wavelength gives a wider pattern."),
        EX("Worked example 1: light in glass", intro=r"Light of wavelength $600\ \text{nm}$ in a vacuum enters glass of refractive index $1.50$. Find its speed, frequency and wavelength in the glass. Take $c = 3.00\times10^8\ \text{m s}^{-1}$.", steps=[
            S("Speed", math=r"v = \frac{c}{n} = \frac{3.00\times10^8}{1.50} = 2.00\times10^8\ \text{m s}^{-1}"),
            S("Frequency (unchanged across the boundary)", math=r"f = \frac{c}{\lambda} = \frac{3.00\times10^8}{600\times10^{-9}} = 5.0\times10^{14}\ \text{Hz}"),
            S("Wavelength in glass", math=r"\lambda_g = \frac{v}{f} = \frac{2.00\times10^8}{5.0\times10^{14}} = 400\ \text{nm}"),
        ], check=r"600/1.5 = 400"),
        EX("Worked example 2: ripples in a tank", intro=r"Ripples travel at $0.24\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ in deep water and $0.18\ \text{m s}^{-1}$ in shallow water. A plane wavefront meets the boundary with an angle of incidence $40^\circ$. Find the angle of refraction.", steps=[
            S("Use $\\sin i/\\sin r = v_1/v_2$", math=r"\sin r = \sin 40^\circ \times \frac{0.18}{0.24} = 0.6428 \times 0.75 = 0.4821"),
            S("Angle", math=r"r = 28.8^\circ"),
            S("Check direction", text="The wave slows down, so it bends towards the normal: r is smaller than i, as found."),
        ], check=r"\arcsin 0.4821 = 28.8^\circ"),
        EX("Worked example 3: single-slit diffraction", intro=r"Light of wavelength $600\ \text{nm}$ falls on a slit of width $0.10\ \text{mm}$. A screen is $2.0\ \text{m}$ away. Find the angle to the first minimum and the width of the central maximum.", steps=[
            S("First minimum", math=r"\sin\theta = \frac{\lambda}{a} = \frac{600\times10^{-9}}{0.10\times10^{-3}} = 6.0\times10^{-3}\Rightarrow \theta = 6.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{rad}"),
            S("Half-width on the screen", math=r"y = D\theta = 2.0 \times 6.0\times10^{-3} = 0.012\ \text{m}"),
            S("Full width of the central maximum", math=r"2y = 0.024\ \text{m} = 24\ \text{mm}"),
        ], check=r"2 \times 2.0 \times 0.006 = 0.024"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Saying the frequency changes in refraction", r"The frequency is fixed by the source. Speed and wavelength change together, $v = f\lambda$, but $f$ stays the same."),
        MISTAKE("Swapping the ratio", r"$\dfrac{\sin i}{\sin r} = \dfrac{v_1}{v_2}$: speed in the **first** medium over the second. In terms of indices, $\dfrac{\sin i}{\sin r} = \dfrac{n_2}{n_1}$."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking a wider slit gives a wider pattern", r"The central maximum has half-angle about $\lambda/a$, so it **narrows** as $a$ increases and widens as the slit narrows."),
        MISTAKE("Using two lamps for an interference pattern", "Two independent lamps are not coherent, so no steady pattern forms. Light must be split from one source."),
        QUIZ("A wave passes from deep to shallow water. Which quantity does not change?",
             ["Speed", "Wavelength", "Frequency", "Direction of travel"], 2,
             r"Frequency is fixed by the source. Speed and wavelength both fall in shallow water, and the direction changes unless the wave is along the normal.",
             hint="What fixes the number of crests arriving each second?",
             feedback=["The speed is lower in shallow water.", "Wavelength falls with speed since f is fixed.", "", "A wave at an angle to the boundary bends."]),
        QUIZ(r"Light of wavelength $500\ \text{nm}$ passes through a slit of width $0.20\ \text{mm}$. What is the angle to the first minimum?",
             [r"$2.5\times10^{-3}\ \text{rad}$", r"$0.20\ \text{rad}$", r"$4.0\times10^{2}\ \text{rad}$", r"$1.25\times10^{-3}\ \text{rad}$"], 0,
             r"$\sin\theta = \dfrac{\lambda}{a} = \dfrac{5.0\times10^{-7}}{2.0\times10^{-4}} = 2.5\times10^{-3}$.", hint=r"Use $a\sin\theta = \lambda$ and convert both lengths to metres.",
             feedback=["", "That mixes up the units (mm and nm).", "That inverts the ratio a/λ.", "That is λ/2a: the formula is a sin θ = λ, not λ/2."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Light of wavelength $560\ \text{nm}$ in air enters water of refractive index $1.33$. Find its wavelength and speed in water. ($c = 3.00\times10^8\ \text{m s}^{-1}$)",
           r"$\lambda_w = \dfrac{560}{1.33} = 421\ \text{nm}$. $v = \dfrac{c}{n} = \dfrac{3.00\times10^8}{1.33} = 2.26\times10^8\ \text{m s}^{-1}$. The frequency stays $5.4\times10^{14}\ \text{Hz}$."),
        PQ(r"Use a Huygens construction (describe it) to show that the angle of reflection equals the angle of incidence.",
           r"A plane wavefront AB meets a mirror, with A touching first. While the edge at B travels BC to the mirror, the wavelet from A spreads into a semicircle of the same radius BC (the speed is unchanged). The tangent from C to this semicircle is the reflected wavefront. Triangles ABC and ADC (D the tangent point) are right-angled, share the hypotenuse AC and have AD = BC, so they are congruent. Hence angle BAC = angle ACD, which gives the angle of incidence equal to the angle of reflection."),
        PQ(r"A slit of width $0.10\ \text{mm}$ is illuminated with light of wavelength $600\ \text{nm}$. How does the central maximum change if the slit width is halved?",
           r"The half-angle $\theta \approx \dfrac{\lambda}{a}$ doubles from $6.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{rad}$ to $1.2\times10^{-2}\ \text{rad}$, so the central maximum becomes twice as wide (and dimmer, since less light passes)."),
        PQ("State the conditions for sustained two-source interference of light and explain why two torches do not show an interference pattern.",
           "The sources must be coherent: same frequency and a constant phase difference, and the waves must have comparable amplitudes (and, for light, the same polarisation). Two torches emit light in random bursts with changing phase, so the phase difference at any point changes randomly in about 10 to the minus 8 seconds and the pattern averages to uniform brightness."),
        TIP("In the exam: Huygens' principle appears as a drawn construction (Paper 2) and as questions about what changes when light enters glass (speed, wavelength, not frequency). Mention coherence whenever you discuss interference."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("Huygens: each point on a wavefront is a source of wavelets; the new wavefront is their envelope.",
            r"Refraction: $\dfrac{\sin i}{\sin r} = \dfrac{v_1}{v_2} = \dfrac{n_2}{n_1}$; frequency is unchanged; $n = \dfrac{c}{v}$.",
            r"Superposition: resultant displacement is the sum. Constructive: $\Delta = m\lambda$; destructive: $\Delta = (m + \tfrac12)\lambda$. Sources must be coherent.",
            r"Single slit: first minimum $a\sin\theta = \lambda$; the central maximum is widest for a narrow slit.",
            "Diffraction is strongest when the gap is about one wavelength."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 13. Young's double slit
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Huygens' principle and Young's double-slit interference",
    title="Young's double-slit experiment",
    slug="phl2-youngs-double-slit",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Derive the fringe spacing w = λD/a from the path difference a sin θ, use it to find wavelength, and predict how fringes change with λ, a, D and white light.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive the path difference a sin θ ≈ ay/D for two slits",
        "Derive and use w = λD/a",
        "Describe the experiment and how λ is measured",
        "Predict changes to the pattern when λ, a or D changes, or white light is used",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Double slit", "Path difference", "Fringe spacing", "Coherent sources", "Bright and dark fringes", "White-light fringes"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"In 1801 Thomas Young showed that light behaves as a wave by making it **interfere**. A modern school laboratory does this with a laser pointer and a double-slit slide. The experiment is simple, and the measurements give the wavelength of light with a metre rule. Light of wavelength less than a thousandth of a millimetre can be measured this way."),
        H("The set-up"),
        T(r"Light from a single source (a laser, or a lamp behind a narrow slit) falls on two very narrow parallel slits $S_1$ and $S_2$, a distance $a$ apart. Because both slits are lit by the same wavefront, they are **coherent**: the waves leaving them are in phase. Each slit diffracts the light, so the two beams overlap on a screen at distance $D$. Where the overlapping waves meet, they interfere."),
        IMG("al-phl2-double-slit.svg", alt="Two slits S1 and S2 a distance a apart, a screen at distance D, and a point P at height y above the central point O. Lines from each slit to P show the path difference S2N = a sin theta. A strip of bright and dark fringes is drawn beside the screen.", caption="Figure 1. Geometry of the double-slit experiment (not to scale: a is much smaller than D)."),
        H("Path difference and fringe spacing"),
        T(r"Take a point $P$ at height $y$ above the central point $O$ on the screen. Let $\theta$ be the angle between the line from the midpoint of the slits to $P$ and the axis. Since $D \gg a$, the two lines $S_1P$ and $S_2P$ are almost parallel. Drop a perpendicular from $S_1$ to $S_2P$, meeting at $N$. The path difference is the small distance $S_2N$:"),
        F(r"\Delta = S_2P - S_1P \approx a\sin\theta \approx a\tan\theta = \frac{ay}{D}"),
        T(r"For a **bright fringe** (constructive interference) $\Delta = m\lambda$, with $m = 0, \pm1, \pm2, \dots$ the **order** of the fringe. Then $\dfrac{ay_m}{D} = m\lambda$, so $y_m = \dfrac{m\lambda D}{a}$. The distance between neighbouring bright fringes (the **fringe spacing** $w$) is the difference $y_{m+1} - y_m$:"),
        D(r"For slit separation $a$, screen distance $D$ and wavelength $\lambda$ (with $a \ll D$ and small angles),", title="Fringe spacing", formula=r"w = \frac{\lambda D}{a}",
          note="The dark fringes lie halfway between the bright ones. The fringes are equally spaced. The central fringe (m = 0) is bright, where the path difference is zero."),
        KEY("What changes w", r"Since $w = \dfrac{\lambda D}{a}$:", cards=[
            [r"Larger $\lambda$", "Wider fringes (red fringes are wider than blue ones)."],
            [r"Larger $D$", "Wider fringes: the pattern spreads out farther away."],
            [r"Larger $a$", "Narrower, more closely spaced fringes. Closer slits give wider fringes."],
        ]),
        T(r"**White light.** Each colour has its own $w$. All colours are bright at $m = 0$, so the central fringe is white. For $m \geq 1$ the colours separate, with violet (small $\lambda$) closer to the centre than red. The fringes then overlap and the pattern fades to near white a few fringes from the centre."),
        T(r"**Practical method.** Use a laser pointer (never look directly into the beam). Measure $D$ with a metre rule, $a$ from the slide (or a travelling microscope), and measure across many fringes, for example 10 spacings, then divide by 10 to reduce the reading error. Use $\lambda = \dfrac{wa}{D}$."),
        EX("Worked example 1: predicting the pattern", intro=r"Light of wavelength $600\ \text{nm}$ falls on two slits $0.50\ \text{mm}$ apart. The screen is $1.5\ \text{m}$ away. Find the fringe spacing and the distance from the central fringe to the third bright fringe.", steps=[
            S("Convert to metres", text="$\\lambda = 6.0\\times10^{-7}\\ \\text{m}$, $a = 5.0\\times10^{-4}\\ \\text{m}$."),
            S("Fringe spacing", math=r"w = \frac{\lambda D}{a} = \frac{6.0\times10^{-7} \times 1.5}{5.0\times10^{-4}} = 1.8\times10^{-3}\ \text{m} = 1.8\ \text{mm}"),
            S("Third bright fringe", math=r"y_3 = 3w = 5.4\ \text{mm}"),
        ], check=r"6\times10^{-7}\times1.5/5\times10^{-4} = 1.8\times10^{-3}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: measuring the wavelength", intro=r"A student measures ten fringe spacings as $12.0\ \text{mm}$. The slit separation is $0.60\ \text{mm}$ and the screen is $1.20\ \text{m}$ from the slits. Find the wavelength.", steps=[
            S("One fringe spacing", math=r"w = \frac{12.0}{10} = 1.20\ \text{mm}"),
            S("Rearrange", math=r"\lambda = \frac{wa}{D} = \frac{1.20\times10^{-3} \times 0.60\times10^{-3}}{1.20} = 6.0\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Answer", text="The wavelength is 600 nm (orange-red light)."),
        ], check=r"1.2\times10^{-3}\times6\times10^{-4}/1.2 = 6.0\times10^{-7}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: white-light fringes", intro=r"Use the set-up of Example 1 ($a = 0.50\ \text{mm}$, $D = 1.5\ \text{m}$) with white light from $400\ \text{nm}$ (violet) to $700\ \text{nm}$ (red). Find the width of the first-order spectrum.", steps=[
            S("Position of the first-order violet fringe", math=r"y_V = \frac{400\times10^{-9} \times 1.5}{0.50\times10^{-3}} = 1.2\ \text{mm}"),
            S("Position of the first-order red fringe", math=r"y_R = \frac{700\times10^{-9} \times 1.5}{0.50\times10^{-3}} = 2.1\ \text{mm}"),
            S("Width", math=r"2.1 - 1.2 = 0.9\ \text{mm}"),
        ], check=r"(700 - 400)\times10^{-9}\times1.5/5\times10^{-4} = 9.0\times10^{-4}"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using centimetres or millimetres directly", r"Convert $a$ and $\lambda$ to metres. A common error is $a = 0.50$ instead of $0.50\times10^{-3}$, which makes $w$ a thousand times too small."),
        MISTAKE("Using w as the distance from the centre to the first fringe for a dark fringe", r"The first **dark** fringe is at $\dfrac{w}{2}$ from the centre, since dark fringes lie midway between the bright ones. The first **bright** fringe is at $w$."),
        MISTAKE("Saying closer slits give closer fringes", r"$w \propto \dfrac{1}{a}$. **Closer** slits give **wider** fringes."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting that the slits must be narrow", "Each slit must be very narrow. Narrow slits diffract the light into broad beams that overlap on the screen. Wide slits give two separate bright patches with no fringes."),
        QUIZ(r"In a double-slit experiment the screen is moved to twice the distance from the slits. What happens to the fringe spacing?",
             ["It is halved.", "It is doubled.", "It is unchanged.", "It is quadrupled."], 1,
             r"$w = \dfrac{\lambda D}{a}$ is proportional to $D$.", hint="Look at the formula.",
             feedback=["That would happen if D were halved.", "", "w depends on D.", "w is proportional to D, not D squared."]),
        QUIZ(r"Red light ($700\ \text{nm}$) is replaced by blue light ($450\ \text{nm}$) in the same arrangement. What happens to the fringes?",
             ["They become wider.", "They become narrower.", "They stay the same.", "They disappear."], 1,
             r"$w \propto \lambda$ and blue has the shorter wavelength.", hint="Compare the wavelengths.",
             feedback=["That would be for a longer wavelength.", "", "w is proportional to wavelength.", "Blue light also interferes."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A laser pointer of wavelength $650\ \text{nm}$ shines through a double slit of separation $0.25\ \text{mm}$ onto a wall $2.0\ \text{m}$ away. Find the fringe spacing.",
           r"$w = \dfrac{\lambda D}{a} = \dfrac{6.5\times10^{-7} \times 2.0}{2.5\times10^{-4}} = 5.2\times10^{-3}\ \text{m} = 5.2\ \text{mm}$."),
        PQ(r"In an experiment with $D = 2.0\ \text{m}$ and $\lambda = 650\ \text{nm}$, eleven bright fringes (ten spacings) span $24\ \text{mm}$. Find the slit separation.",
           r"$w = 2.4\ \text{mm}$. $a = \dfrac{\lambda D}{w} = \dfrac{6.5\times10^{-7} \times 2.0}{2.4\times10^{-3}} = 5.4\times10^{-4}\ \text{m} = 0.54\ \text{mm}$."),
        PQ(r"Show that the path difference from the two slits to a point at height $y$ on the screen is approximately $\dfrac{ay}{D}$, and deduce the fringe spacing.",
           r"With $D \gg a$ the rays $S_1P$ and $S_2P$ are nearly parallel, so $S_2P - S_1P \approx a\sin\theta$, where $\theta$ is the angle to the axis. For small angles $\sin\theta \approx \tan\theta = \dfrac{y}{D}$, so $\Delta \approx \dfrac{ay}{D}$. A bright fringe needs $\Delta = m\lambda$, giving $y_m = \dfrac{m\lambda D}{a}$. Successive bright fringes differ by $w = \dfrac{\lambda D}{a}$."),
        PQ(r"A thin transparent sheet of thickness $0.010\ \text{mm}$ and refractive index $1.5$ is placed over one slit in the experiment with $\lambda = 600\ \text{nm}$, $a = 0.50\ \text{mm}$, $D = 1.5\ \text{m}$. By how many fringes does the pattern shift?",
           r"The sheet adds an optical path $(n - 1)t = 0.5 \times 1.0\times10^{-5} = 5.0\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}$ for that beam, which is $\dfrac{5.0\times10^{-6}}{6.0\times10^{-7}} = 8.3$ wavelengths. The pattern shifts by about $8.3$ fringes, that is $8.3 \times 1.8 = 15\ \text{mm}$, towards the covered slit."),
        TIP("In the exam: the double-slit formula is tested in Paper 1 (how w changes) and in Paper 2 (the derivation, the calculation and the experimental method). In the practical Paper 3 expect measurements over several fringes. Convert all lengths to metres."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Coherent light from two slits gives bright fringes where $\Delta = m\lambda$ and dark fringes where $\Delta = (m + \tfrac12)\lambda$.",
            r"$\Delta \approx a\sin\theta \approx \dfrac{ay}{D}$ and fringe spacing $w = \dfrac{\lambda D}{a}$ (valid for $a \ll D$).",
            r"Bigger $\lambda$ or $D$ widens the fringes; bigger $a$ narrows them.",
            "White light: central white fringe, then coloured fringes (violet nearest the centre) that overlap.",
            "Measure across many fringes to find the wavelength accurately."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 14. Diffraction gratings
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Diffraction gratings",
    title="The diffraction grating",
    slug="phl2-diffraction-grating",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Derive d sin θ = nλ for a diffraction grating, find line spacing, orders and angles for given wavelengths, and explain white-light spectra and order overlap.",
    objectives=[
        "Derive d sin θ = nλ and convert lines per millimetre to d",
        "Find the angles and the maximum number of orders for a given grating",
        "Describe the spectrum of white light and the overlap of orders",
        "Explain why a grating gives sharper lines than a double slit and state the resolving power nN",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Diffraction grating", "Grating spacing d", "Order n", "Maximum order", "Spectrum", "Order overlap", "Resolving power"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"A compact disc held in sunlight splits it into colours. The rainbow patterns come from thousands of tiny, equally spaced grooves acting together as a **diffraction grating**. Scientists use real gratings to separate light into its colours and to measure wavelengths very accurately, from the gases in a laboratory discharge tube to the light of distant stars."),
        D(r"A **diffraction grating** is a plate with a very large number of equally spaced parallel slits (or lines). The distance between the centres of neighbouring slits is the **grating spacing** $d$. If the grating has $N_0$ lines per millimetre, $d = \dfrac{1\ \text{mm}}{N_0} = \dfrac{10^{-3}}{N_0}\ \text{m}$.", title="Diffraction grating"),
        H("The grating equation"),
        IMG("al-phl2-grating-orders.svg", alt="A beam of light meets a grating at normal incidence. Transmitted beams leave in the central direction (order 0) and at equal angles on both sides for orders plus and minus 1 and plus and minus 2. The angle of the first order is marked.", caption="Figure 1. Orders from a grating at normal incidence. Here λ/d = 0.35, so there is no third order."),
        T(r"Light of wavelength $\lambda$ falls normally on the grating. Waves from two neighbouring slits leave at angle $\theta$ to the normal. The extra distance travelled by the wave from the lower slit is $d\sin\theta$, the path difference, as for the double slit. If this equals a whole number of wavelengths, **all** the slits send waves that arrive in phase, because the next slit adds another $d\sin\theta$:"),
        F(r"d\sin\theta = n\lambda, \qquad n = 0, \pm1, \pm2, \dots"),
        T(r"$n$ is the **order**. $n = 0$ is the straight-through beam. The largest possible $n$ comes from $\sin\theta \leq 1$, so $n_{max}$ is the largest whole number not exceeding $\dfrac{d}{\lambda}$."),
        T(r"**Why the lines are sharp.** With two slits the bright fringes are broad. With many slits, away from an exact maximum, waves from different slits arrive with all sorts of phases and cancel almost completely. So the bright lines are narrow and bright. The more lines are illuminated, the sharper the lines."),
        H("White light and spectra"),
        T(r"In white light every wavelength has its own angle for each order $n \geq 1$: $\sin\theta = \dfrac{n\lambda}{d}$. Violet (short $\lambda$) is deviated least and red most. The zeroth order is white for all colours. Each order $n \geq 1$ is a **spectrum**, a band of colours from violet to red. Higher orders are wider and may **overlap** with neighbours: the third-order violet appears at a smaller angle than the second-order red whenever $3\lambda_V < 2\lambda_R$. For $400\ \text{nm}$ and $700\ \text{nm}$, $1200 < 1400$, so the second and third orders overlap."),
        CODE("""Order n   sin(theta) = n x 600 nm / d   theta   (d = 3.333 micrometre, 300 lines/mm)
0         0.000                         0.00 deg
1         0.180                         10.37 deg
2         0.360                         21.10 deg
3         0.540                         32.68 deg
4         0.720                         46.05 deg
5         0.900                         64.16 deg
(6 would need sin(theta) = 1.08, impossible: 5 orders each side, 11 beams in all)""", "text", title="Orders for 600 nm light on a 300 lines/mm grating (computed)"),
        T(r"**Resolving power.** The ability to separate two close wavelengths $\lambda$ and $\lambda + \Delta\lambda$ is $R = \dfrac{\lambda}{\Delta\lambda}$. For a grating in order $n$ with $N$ lines illuminated, $R = nN$. To separate the sodium doublet ($589.0\ \text{nm}$ and $589.6\ \text{nm}$, $\Delta\lambda = 0.6\ \text{nm}$) in the first order you need $N \geq \dfrac{589.3}{0.6} \approx 980$ lines. (Textbooks differ in how they define resolving power; check your own text.)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: angles and orders", intro=r"A grating has $300$ lines per millimetre. Light of wavelength $600\ \text{nm}$ falls normally on it. Find the grating spacing, the angle of the first order and the number of orders seen.", steps=[
            S("Grating spacing", math=r"d = \frac{10^{-3}}{300} = 3.33\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}"),
            S("First order", math=r"\sin\theta_1 = \frac{\lambda}{d} = \frac{6.00\times10^{-7}}{3.33\times10^{-6}} = 0.18 \Rightarrow \theta_1 = 10.4^\circ"),
            S("Maximum order", math=r"\frac{d}{\lambda} = 5.56 \Rightarrow n_{max} = 5"),
            S("Total number of beams", text="Orders from −5 to +5, that is 11 beams, including the central one. The fifth order is at 64.2 degrees."),
        ], check=r"\sin\theta_5 = 5 \times 0.18 = 0.90 \Rightarrow 64.2^\circ"),
        EX("Worked example 2: measuring a wavelength", intro=r"Light from a lamp passes through a grating with $400$ lines per millimetre. A bright line is seen in the second order at $33.0^\circ$ from the central beam. Find the wavelength.", steps=[
            S("Spacing", math=r"d = \frac{10^{-3}}{400} = 2.50\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Rearrange $d\\sin\\theta = n\\lambda$", math=r"\lambda = \frac{d\sin\theta}{n} = \frac{2.50\times10^{-6} \times \sin 33.0^\circ}{2} = 6.81\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}"),
            S("Answer", text="About 681 nm (red light)."),
        ], check=r"2.5\times10^{-6} \times 0.5446/2 = 6.81\times10^{-7}"),
        EX("Worked example 3: the spectrum of white light", intro=r"White light (from $400\ \text{nm}$ to $700\ \text{nm}$) falls on a $500$ lines per millimetre grating. Find the angular width of the first-order spectrum and decide whether the second and third orders overlap.", steps=[
            S("Spacing", math=r"d = 2.00\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}"),
            S("First order, violet and red", math=r"\theta_V = \arcsin\frac{400}{2000} = 11.5^\circ,\quad \theta_R = \arcsin\frac{700}{2000} = 20.5^\circ"),
            S("Width of the first-order spectrum", math=r"20.5^\circ - 11.5^\circ = 9.0^\circ"),
            S("Second order covers", math=r"\arcsin 0.40 = 23.6^\circ \ \text{to}\ \arcsin 0.70 = 44.4^\circ"),
            S("Third order starts at violet", math=r"\arcsin 0.60 = 36.9^\circ"),
            S("Conclusion", text="The third-order violet (36.9 degrees) is inside the second-order spectrum, which extends to 44.4 degrees, so the second and third orders overlap. The first and second orders do not overlap (20.5 < 23.6)."),
        ], check=r"3 \times 400 = 1200 < 2 \times 700 = 1400"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Using lines per millimetre as d", r"$d$ is the **reciprocal**, in metres. 300 lines per mm gives $d = \dfrac{1}{300}\ \text{mm} = 3.33\ \mu\text{m}$, not $300$."),
        MISTAKE("Stating a non-integer maximum order", r"If $d/\lambda = 5.56$, the maximum order is $5$ (round down). Never round up: $n = 6$ would need $\sin\theta > 1$."),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting both sides", "For each order n there is a beam on each side of the centre. Count 2n_max + 1 beams including the central beam."),
        MISTAKE("Saying red is deviated least", r"$\sin\theta = n\lambda/d$ grows with $\lambda$. Red (longest $\lambda$) is deviated **most**, violet least. This is the opposite of a prism, which deviates violet most."),
        QUIZ(r"A grating has $200$ lines per millimetre. What is the grating spacing?",
             [r"$5.0\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}$", r"$2.0\times10^{-4}\ \text{m}$", r"$200\ \text{m}$", r"$5.0\times10^{-3}\ \text{m}$"], 0,
             r"$d = \dfrac{10^{-3}}{200} = 5.0\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}$.", hint="Take the reciprocal and convert mm to m.",
             feedback=["", "That multiplies instead of dividing: the spacing is 1 mm divided by 200.", "That ignores the unit conversion and the reciprocal.", "That is 1/200 of a millimetre written as 5 mm per thousand: a slip of three powers of ten."]),
        QUIZ(r"Light of wavelength $600\ \text{nm}$ falls normally on a grating of spacing $2.5\ \mu\text{m}$. How many beams are seen in all, counting both sides and the central beam?",
             ["4", "7", "9", "5"], 2,
             r"$\dfrac{d}{\lambda} = \dfrac{2.5}{0.6} = 4.17$, so $n_{max} = 4$. Orders run from $-4$ to $+4$: 9 beams.",
             hint="Find d/λ, round down, then count both sides.",
             feedback=["That is n_max, the number of orders on one side only.", "That would need n_max = 3.", "", "An odd total is needed, but 5 would mean n_max = 2."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"A grating has $600$ lines per millimetre. Laser light of wavelength $650\ \text{nm}$ falls on it normally. Find the angles of all the orders.",
           r"$d = \dfrac{10^{-3}}{600} = 1.667\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}$. $\dfrac{d}{\lambda} = 2.56$, so $n_{max} = 2$. $\sin\theta_1 = \dfrac{650}{1667} = 0.390$, $\theta_1 = 23.0^\circ$. $\sin\theta_2 = 0.780$, $\theta_2 = 51.3^\circ$. Beams at $0^\circ$, $\pm23.0^\circ$, $\pm51.3^\circ$: five beams."),
        PQ(r"A grating gives a first-order maximum at $17.5^\circ$ for sodium light of wavelength $589\ \text{nm}$. Find the number of lines per millimetre.",
           r"$d = \dfrac{\lambda}{\sin\theta} = \dfrac{5.89\times10^{-7}}{\sin 17.5^\circ} = 1.96\times10^{-6}\ \text{m}$. Lines per mm $= \dfrac{10^{-3}}{1.96\times10^{-6}} = 511$, about $510$ lines per mm."),
        PQ(r"Explain why the third-order spectrum of white light overlaps the second-order spectrum, using wavelengths of $400\ \text{nm}$ and $700\ \text{nm}$.",
           r"At the same angle, $n\lambda$ is the same for both orders: $3\lambda_1 = 2\lambda_2$. The start of the third-order spectrum (violet, $3 \times 400 = 1200\ \text{nm}$) is at a smaller angle than the end of the second-order spectrum (red, $2 \times 700 = 1400\ \text{nm}$). So third-order violet lies within the second-order spectrum, and the two overlap. The first and second orders do not overlap because $2 \times 400 = 800 > 700$."),
        PQ(r"State two advantages of a diffraction grating over a double slit for measuring wavelength.",
           "The maxima are much sharper (narrow lines against broad fringes) so the angles can be measured more precisely, and they are brighter, so the pattern is easy to see. Using larger angles and higher orders also gives a smaller percentage uncertainty in the wavelength."),
        TIP("In the exam: grating calculations (d sin θ = nλ, maximum order, white-light overlap) appear in Paper 1 and Paper 2, and the grating is a standard spectrometer experiment in the practical Paper 3. Show d in metres and round the maximum order DOWN."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM(r"Grating spacing $d = \dfrac{1}{\text{lines per metre}}$; maxima at $d\sin\theta = n\lambda$.",
            r"Maximum order: largest integer $n \leq \dfrac{d}{\lambda}$; beams appear on both sides of the central beam.",
            "White light: white central beam, spectra in higher orders, violet nearest the centre; neighbouring higher orders can overlap (3 × violet < 2 × red).",
            "Many slits give narrow, bright, sharp maxima, so a grating measures wavelengths accurately.",
            r"Resolving power $R = \dfrac{\lambda}{\Delta\lambda} = nN$ (check your textbook for the definition)."),
    ],
))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 15. Polarization
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2,
    topic="Polarization",
    title="Polarization of light: Malus's law and Brewster's angle",
    slug="phl2-polarization",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Explain why polarization shows that light is a transverse wave, use Malus's law I = I0 cos²θ and Brewster's law tan θB = n, and describe uses such as polaroid sunglasses.",
    objectives=[
        "Define plane-polarised light and explain what polarization shows about light",
        "Use Malus's law for a polariser and analyser, and for several polaroids",
        "Use Brewster's law to find the angle at which reflected light is fully polarised",
        "Describe two ways to produce polarised light and some uses",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Transverse wave", "Plane polarised light", "Polaroid", "Malus's law", "Brewster's angle", "Polarisation by reflection", "Unpolarised light"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T(r"Drivers on a bright afternoon on the Douala road often wear polaroid sunglasses to cut glare from wet roads and car bonnets. Tilt your head and the glare changes. This effect shows something important: **light is a transverse wave**, and in a transverse wave the oscillations can be in different directions."),
        H("What is polarization?"),
        D(r"**Polarization** is the restriction of the oscillations of a transverse wave to a single plane. In **plane-polarised light** the electric field oscillates in only one direction, at right angles to the direction of travel. Ordinary (**unpolarised**) light, from the Sun or a lamp, has oscillations in all directions at right angles to the beam.", title="Plane-polarised light"),
        T(r"Only **transverse** waves can be polarised. Sound in air is longitudinal, so it cannot be polarised: that is how polarization proves that light is transverse. Imagine a rope passing through a vertical slot in a fence. Vertical waves go through; horizontal waves are stopped."),
        IMG("al-phl2-polarisers.svg", alt="Unpolarised light passes through a polariser and becomes plane polarised with half its intensity. A second polariser, the analyser, has its axis at angle theta to the first. The light leaving it has intensity I1 cos squared theta.", caption="Figure 1. A polariser and an analyser. Unpolarised light loses half its intensity at the polariser."),
        H("Polaroid and Malus's law"),
        T(r"A **polaroid** sheet has a **transmission axis**. It lets through only the component of the electric field along that axis. Unpolarised light of intensity $I_0$ passing through a polaroid emerges plane-polarised with intensity $\dfrac{I_0}{2}$ (on average half the oscillation energy is along any given axis)."),
        T(r"Now let plane-polarised light of amplitude $E_1$ and intensity $I_1$ meet a second polaroid (the **analyser**) with its axis at angle $\theta$ to the polarisation direction. The component of the electric field along the axis is $E_1\cos\theta$. Intensity is proportional to the amplitude squared, so"),
        D(r"If plane-polarised light of intensity $I_1$ falls on an analyser whose axis makes angle $\theta$ with the plane of polarization,", title="Malus's law", formula=r"I = I_1\cos^2\theta",
          note=r"At $\theta = 0$ all light passes; at $\theta = 90^\circ$ (crossed polaroids) none passes."),
        H("Polarization by reflection: Brewster's law"),
        T(r"Light reflected from glass, water or a polished road is partly polarised, with oscillations mostly parallel to the surface. At one special angle of incidence, the **Brewster angle** $\theta_B$, the reflected light is **completely** plane-polarised. This happens when the reflected and refracted rays are at $90^\circ$ to each other. Applying Snell's law to this situation (with $\theta_B + r = 90^\circ$, so $\sin r = \cos\theta_B$):"),
        F(r"n = \frac{\sin\theta_B}{\sin r} = \frac{\sin\theta_B}{\cos\theta_B} = \tan\theta_B"),
        D(r"The reflected light from a surface of refractive index $n$ (in air) is completely plane-polarised when the angle of incidence $\theta_B$ satisfies", title="Brewster's law", formula=r"\tan\theta_B = n"),
        T(r"Polaroid sunglasses have a **vertical** transmission axis. Glare from a horizontal surface is mostly polarised horizontally, so the lenses block it while letting through the useful unpolarised light."),
        H("Uses"),
        KEY("Where polarization is used", "", cards=[
            ["Sunglasses and camera filters", "Reduce glare from water, glass and roads."],
            ["Stress analysis", "Transparent plastic models between crossed polaroids show coloured fringes where the material is under stress."],
            ["Liquid crystal displays", "A calculator or phone screen uses polarised light and liquid crystals that turn the polarization on and off."],
            ["Optical activity", "Sugar solutions rotate the plane of polarization, and the rotation measures the sugar concentration."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: two polaroids", intro=r"Unpolarised light of intensity $I_0$ passes through a polariser and then an analyser whose axis is at $30^\circ$ to the polariser's axis. Find the final intensity.", steps=[
            S("After the polariser", math=r"I_1 = \frac{I_0}{2}"),
            S("After the analyser (Malus)", math=r"I_2 = I_1\cos^2 30^\circ = \frac{I_0}{2} \times 0.75 = 0.375\,I_0"),
        ], check=r"\cos^2 30^\circ = 0.75"),
        EX("Worked example 2: a third polaroid", intro=r"Two polaroids are crossed at $90^\circ$ so no light gets through. A third polaroid, with axis at $45^\circ$ to both, is placed between them. Find the transmitted fraction of unpolarised light of intensity $I_0$.", steps=[
            S("First polaroid", math=r"I_1 = \frac{I_0}{2}"),
            S("Middle polaroid ($45^\\circ$)", math=r"I_2 = I_1\cos^2 45^\circ = \frac{I_0}{2} \times \frac12 = \frac{I_0}{4}"),
            S("Last polaroid ($45^\\circ$ again)", math=r"I_3 = I_2\cos^2 45^\circ = \frac{I_0}{4} \times \frac12 = \frac{I_0}{8}"),
            S("Comment", text="Adding a polaroid between crossed ones lets 12.5% of the light through. A polaroid does not only remove light: it rotates the plane of polarization.")
        ], check=r"\tfrac12 \times \tfrac12 \times \tfrac12 = 0.125"),
        EX("Worked example 3: Brewster's angle", intro=r"Find the Brewster angle for light reflecting from (a) glass of $n = 1.50$ and (b) water of $n = 1.33$ in air. For glass, what is the angle of refraction at this incidence?", steps=[
            S("(a) Glass", math=r"\theta_B = \arctan 1.50 = 56.3^\circ"),
            S("(b) Water", math=r"\theta_B = \arctan 1.33 = 53.1^\circ"),
            S("Refraction angle in glass", math=r"r = 90^\circ - 56.3^\circ = 33.7^\circ"),
            S("Check with Snell's law", math=r"\frac{\sin 56.3^\circ}{\sin 33.7^\circ} = \frac{0.832}{0.555} = 1.50"),
        ], check=r"\tan 56.31^\circ = 1.500"),
        H("Common mistakes"),
        MISTAKE("Forgetting the factor of one half", r"Unpolarised light loses half its intensity at the first polaroid. Malus's law applies only to already polarised light. Do not write $I = I_0\cos^2\theta$ for unpolarised $I_0$."),
        MISTAKE("Confusing cos² with cos", r"Intensity is proportional to the **square** of the amplitude, so $I \propto \cos^2\theta$, not $\cos\theta$."),
        MISTAKE("Saying longitudinal waves can be polarised", "Polarization is only possible for transverse waves, so sound waves in air cannot be polarised."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the Brewster angle with the critical angle", r"Brewster: $\tan\theta_B = n$, partial reflection polarised completely. Critical angle: $\sin\theta_c = \dfrac{1}{n}$, total internal reflection. They are different effects with different angles."),
        QUIZ(r"Plane-polarised light of intensity $I_1$ falls on an analyser at $60^\circ$ to its plane of polarization. What is the transmitted intensity?",
             [r"$0.50\,I_1$", r"$0.25\,I_1$", r"$0.75\,I_1$", r"$0.87\,I_1$"], 1,
             r"$I = I_1\cos^2 60^\circ = I_1 \times 0.25$.", hint=r"Square the cosine: $\cos 60^\circ = 0.5$.",
             feedback=["That uses cos 60 without squaring.", "", "That is cos² 30°, the value for the complementary angle.", "That is cos 30°, the amplitude factor for the wrong angle."]),
        QUIZ("Which of these waves can be polarised?",
             ["Sound in air", "Waves on a stretched rope", "Compression waves in a slinky spring", "Sound in water"], 1,
             "Only transverse waves can be polarised. Waves on a rope are transverse; the other three are longitudinal.", hint="Which wave has oscillations at right angles to the direction of travel?",
             feedback=["Sound in air is longitudinal.", "", "A compression wave is longitudinal.", "Sound in water is longitudinal."]),
        H("Practice questions"),
        PQ(r"Unpolarised light of intensity $80\ \text{W m}^{-2}$ passes through a polariser and an analyser at $45^\circ$ to it. Find the final intensity.",
           r"After the polariser: $\dfrac{80}{2} = 40\ \text{W m}^{-2}$. After the analyser: $40\cos^2 45^\circ = 40 \times 0.5 = 20\ \text{W m}^{-2}$."),
        PQ(r"Light reflecting from a pond ($n = 1.33$) is completely polarised. Find the angle of incidence and the angle between the reflected and refracted rays.",
           r"$\theta_B = \arctan 1.33 = 53.1^\circ$. The reflected and refracted rays are at $90^\circ$ to each other."),
        PQ(r"Explain why a driver wearing polaroid sunglasses sees less glare from a wet road, and why the glare returns if she tilts her head sideways.",
           r"Light reflected from the wet road is mostly polarised horizontally. Polaroid lenses with a vertical axis block the horizontal oscillations, so most of the glare is removed. If she tilts her head by $90^\circ$ the axes become horizontal, so they transmit the polarised glare, which returns."),
        PQ(r"The intensity of polarised light falls to $\tfrac14$ of its value after passing through an analyser. What is the angle between the analyser axis and the plane of polarization?",
           r"$\cos^2\theta = \tfrac14 \Rightarrow \cos\theta = 0.5 \Rightarrow \theta = 60^\circ$."),
        TIP("In the exam: Malus's law, the factor of one half for unpolarised light and the idea that only transverse waves polarise are common in Paper 1; Paper 2 may ask for polarization by reflection and Brewster's law. Tell the examiner what polarization shows about the nature of light."),
        H("Summary"),
        SUM("Polarization restricts a transverse wave to one plane; only transverse waves can be polarised, so it shows that light is transverse.",
            r"Unpolarised light through one polaroid: $I = \dfrac{I_0}{2}$. Polarised light through an analyser: $I = I_1\cos^2\theta$ (Malus's law).",
            r"Brewster's law: reflected light is fully polarised when $\tan\theta_B = n$; the reflected and refracted rays are then at $90^\circ$.",
            "Polaroid sunglasses (vertical axis) cut the horizontally polarised glare from surfaces.",
            "Uses: sunglasses, stress analysis, liquid crystal displays and measuring sugar concentration."),
    ],
))
